// Shared cloud view-march library (fork — 2026-06-10).
//
// Extracted from cloud_render.comp.slang so the secondary-ray cloud LUT
// bake (cloud_secondary_lut.comp.slang) can run the exact same slab march
// + Nubis Cubed shading as the per-pixel view pass. Three pieces live here:
//
//   * CloudShadeContext + buildCloudShadeContext — the static-per-frame
//     shading state (sun/sky/moon precomputes). Despite originally living
//     in cloud_render's main(), every input is pixel-INDEPENDENT, so both
//     passes build it once per thread from the args CB alone.
//   * sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth_local — the 2-tap moon-shadow march.
//   * marchCloudSlab / marchCloudLayers — the per-slab raymarch and the
//     layer-1 + optional-layer-2 sequencing. The tMinClamp / tMaxClamp
//     parameters clamp the marched segment to camera distances
//     [tMinClamp, tMaxClamp] (0 disables the respective clamp); passing
//     0/0 reproduces the pre-extraction march bit-for-bit. cloud_render.comp.slang is now the
//     first caller to pass a nonzero tMaxClamp (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration
//     Stage 4a): it clamps the march to the resolved primary-ray surface, using the depth
//     conversion documented at that call site. cloud_secondary_lut.comp.slang still passes 0/0.
//   * marchCloudLayers also reports two depth-companion outputs (Stage 4a): entryDistanceKm (the nearest
//     distance at which EITHER layer found actual cloud material, -1 if neither did; this was
//     "where the layer-1 span started" before 2026-09-06) and meanCloudDepthKm (a transmittance-weighted mean distance
//     through whatever cloud material either layer found), both in km. Threaded down through
//     marchCloudSlab / marchEchoDeck / integrateCloudSample — see the accumulator parameters on
//     each. Only cloud_render.comp.slang's depth companion RT consumes them today.
//
// The dome-direction mapping the LUT bake + sampler share
// (cloudDomeUvToDir / cloudDomeDirToUv) lives in atmosphere_common.slangh
// because the sample side (atmosphere_sky.slangh) does not include this
// header.
//
// Include AFTER atmosphere_common.slangh, in a TU that declared the
// texture globals the helpers reach for (AtmosphereCloudNoise3DSampler,
// AtmosphereSkyViewLut,
// AtmosphereCloudSkyTransmittanceLut, AtmosphereSkyViewSampler) —
// the established binding-then-include layout both consumers use.

// Lightning flash gate (fork — 2026-07-14). The in-cloud flash emissive is a
// TRANSIENT per-frame effect, so only the screen-space cloud pass
// (cloud_render.comp.slang, which #defines this to 1 before including) may
// evaluate it. The secondary-ray cloud LUT (cloud_secondary_lut.comp.slang)
// includes this same header and persists across frames — a flash baked into
// it would linger in reflections until the next re-bake. Default 0 keeps any
// other consumer flash-free by construction.
#ifndef CLOUD_PROFILE_DENSITY_ONLY
#define CLOUD_PROFILE_DENSITY_ONLY 0
#endif
#ifndef CLOUD_PROFILE_NO_MOON_SHADOWS
#define CLOUD_PROFILE_NO_MOON_SHADOWS 0
#endif

#ifndef CLOUD_MARCH_LIGHTNING
#define CLOUD_MARCH_LIGHTNING 0
#endif

// Nubis3 NVDF/SDF density model (fork — Nubis3 conversion Phase B; sole model
// since the legacy retirement, 2026-07-16). Brings in sampleCloudDensityNubis3
// + valueErosion. (Includes atmosphere_common.slangh itself — a no-op here
// since consumers include that first; pragma-once guarded.)
#include "rtx/pass/atmosphere/cloud_nubis3_common.slangh"

// =============================================================================
// Moon-shadow optical-depth tap.
//
// A self-contained 2-tap deterministic march so the cloud-march moon path can
// compute the sun/moon-direction optical depth without pulling in any sky
// header. (This was ported from a `sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth` helper that
// formerly lived in atmosphere_sky.slangh, since removed.) kTaps=2, kStepKm=0.8,
// `density × stepLen` accumulation, with a near-horizon gate. The original
// `pixelCoord` / `frameIdx` jitter params were dropped from the signature
// because they go unused — a deterministic estimate avoids Jensen's-inequality
// bias on the convex exp(-x) shadow estimator.
// =============================================================================
// planetCenterWorldKm / planetRadiusKm (fork - 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage 1;
// ported from archaeology commit 30d20a8f5). The taps walk WORLD-anchored positions (cloudHitPos is
// world-anchored), so they must measure altitude against the world-anchored centre. Supersedes the
// cloudRadiusKm parameter, which named a cloud-only planet that no longer exists (see
// cloudPlanetRadius's retirement in atmosphere_common.slangh).
float sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth_local(vec3 cloudHitPos, vec3 sunDir,
                                        float typeLocal, float coverageLocal,
                                        vec3 planetCenterWorldKm, float planetRadiusKm,
                                        AtmosphereArgs args) {
  if (sunDir.y <= 0.01f) return 0.0f;

  const int   kTaps   = 2;
  const float kStepKm = 0.8f;
  float opticalDepth  = 0.0f;

  for (int i = 0; i < kTaps; ++i) {
    vec3 p = cloudHitPos + sunDir * (kStepKm * float(i + 1));
    float hf = computeCloudHeightFractionC(p, planetCenterWorldKm, planetRadiusKm,
                                           args.cloudAltitude, args.cloudThickness);
    float d = sampleCloudDensityNubis3Coarse(p, hf, typeLocal, coverageLocal, 1.0f, args,
                                             AtmosphereCloudNvdfSdf, AtmosphereCloudDetailNoise3D,
                                             AtmosphereCloudNoise3DSampler);
    opticalDepth += d;
  }

  return opticalDepth * kStepKm;
}

// Slab-parametric variant (fork — 2026-06-21, echo-deck moon). Identical 2-tap
// march, but the density taps compute height fraction against an explicit
// [slabAltitudeKm, slabAltitudeKm + slabThicknessKm] band and apply the slab's
// own density scale. The args-default helper above keys on layer 1's altitude;
// at the echo deck's altitude that reads as "above the slab" (zero density), so
// the deck would self-shadow against nothing. marchEchoDeck calls this so the
// moon silver-lining shadow tracks the DECK's water, not layer 1's.
float sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth_localSlab(vec3 cloudHitPos, vec3 sunDir,
                                           float typeLocal, float coverageLocal,
                                           float slabAltitudeKm, float slabThicknessKm,
                                           float slabDensityScale, vec3 fieldOffsetKm,
                                           vec3 planetCenterWorldKm, float planetRadiusKm,
                                           AtmosphereArgs args) {
  if (sunDir.y <= 0.01f) return 0.0f;

  const int   kTaps   = 2;
  const float kStepKm = 0.8f;
  float opticalDepth  = 0.0f;

  for (int i = 0; i < kTaps; ++i) {
    vec3 p = cloudHitPos + sunDir * (kStepKm * float(i + 1));
    float hf = computeCloudHeightFractionC(p, planetCenterWorldKm, planetRadiusKm, slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm);
    // fieldOffsetKm decorrelates the deck's density FIELD from layer 1 (the
    // echo-deck seed offset) while hf stays on the true position — see the
    // matching tap in marchEchoDeck.
    float d = sampleCloudDensityNubis3Coarse(p + fieldOffsetKm, hf, typeLocal, coverageLocal,
                                             slabDensityScale, args,
                                             AtmosphereCloudNvdfSdf, AtmosphereCloudDetailNoise3D,
                                             AtmosphereCloudNoise3DSampler);
    opticalDepth += d;
  }

  return opticalDepth * kStepKm;
}

// ===========================================================================
// Nubis Cubed 2023 lighting evaluator
// (fork — 2026-05-12, C4; MOVED here from atmosphere_common.slangh and split
// into core + legacy wrapper for the Nubis3 conversion, Phase B. This header
// is compiled only by cloud_render + cloud_secondary_lut, so lighting tweaks
// no longer recompile the path tracer.)
//
// Per-sample lighting equations from the Nubis Cubed 2023 paper (pages 137
// and 142). evalNubisCubedSampleCore takes the modeling inputs
// (dim_profile / cloud_sdf / downTau) as PARAMETERS — the NVDF profile, the
// live SDF, and a D_ambient tap (layer 1) or analytic proxies (echo deck).
//
// Spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-05-12-cloud-lighting-nubis-cubed-design.md
// ===========================================================================

// Henyey-Greenstein phase function — Nubis Cubed flavor (max(denom, 1e-5)
// guard chosen to match the paper's evaluator). The hgPhase() helper in
// atmosphere_common.slangh is a slightly different formulation (it clamps g
// rather than the denominator) — keep both so non-Nubis call sites are
// unperturbed.
float hgPhaseNubis(float cosTheta, float g) {
  float g2 = g * g;
  float denom = 1.0f + g2 - 2.0f * g * cosTheta;
  return (1.0f - g2) / (4.0f * kPi * pow(max(denom, 1e-5f), 1.5f));
}

// =============================================================================
// Shading state shared across both layer marches (and, since the extraction,
// across both march passes). Built once per thread by buildCloudShadeContext.
//
// Two halves (fork — 2026-07-30, cloud march perf):
//   * FRAME-CONSTANT — derived from the args CB alone (the original contents).
//   * RAY-CONSTANT — derived from the args CB plus the pixel's view direction,
//     filled by cloudShadeContextBeginRay. These used to be recomputed inside
//     the per-sample loop even though `viewDirYUp` is fixed for a whole ray:
//     two Henyey-Greenstein evaluations, the sun-dot multi-scatter remap, the
//     powder view fade, the sunset ambient ramp, the energy-conserving lobe
//     weights, and — the worst offender — the Wrenninge moon phase-octave loop,
//     a 3-iteration loop over hgPhase() producing an identical value at every
//     one of the march's 64-96 samples. Every field here is an exact hoist of
//     the expression it replaces (no reassociation), so the render is unchanged.
// =============================================================================
struct CloudShadeContext {
  // ---- Frame-constant ----
  vec3  sunDirYUp;
  vec3  sunRadiance;
  vec3  skyRadianceWarm;     // Sun-direction sky LUT sample (warm at sunset).
  vec3  skyRadianceCool;     // Anti-sun-horizon sky LUT sample (cool at sunset).
                             // Per-sample blend happens in evalNubisCubedSampleCore.
  vec3  skyRadianceZenith;   // Overhead sky LUT sample — the sky-dome underside
                             // fill (bright daytime, dim at sunset). Used by
                             // evalNubisCubedSampleCore's dome-fill term.
  vec3  combinedMoonDir;
  vec3  combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff;
  vec3  combinedMoonPhaseAmt;
  vec3  nightLight;
  float moonBaseSigma;
  uint  msOctaveCount;
  // One planet, held in the two frames the march needs it in (fork - 2026-09-05, world-space cloud
  // migration Stage 1; ported from archaeology commit 30d20a8f5). Replaces cloudRadiusKm, which
  // cached cloudPlanetRadius(args)'s exp() — gone with the fake cloud planet (see its retirement in
  // atmosphere_common.slangh), so there is nothing left to hoist, only frames to keep straight.
  vec3  planetCenterKm;      // getCloudPlanetCenter(args): CAMERA-relative. Shell/planet intersections.
  vec3  planetCenterWorldKm; // getCloudPlanetCenterWorldKm(args): WORLD-anchored. Height fractions,
                             // which travel with the world-anchored density fields.
  float planetRadiusKm;      // args.planetRadius. The atmosphere's radius, not a cloud-only one.
  float darkenStrength;      // Underside-light darkening amount. ZERO through the
                             // whole night / low-sun band, and when it is zero
                             // verticalLight is exactly 1 — which makes the
                             // D_ambient texture tap that feeds it dead work.
  float microAoScale;        // kMicroAoGain * cloudMicroAoStrength.
  float ambientShadowAmt;    // saturate(cloudAmbientShadowStrength).
  float sunsetFactor;        // sunset ambient ramp; 0 above cloudSunsetAmbientRampHighSun,
                             // where the cool-blend exp() is dead (every midday frame).
  float lobeWeightA;         // 1 - msLobeS * msLobeW
  float lobeWeightB;         // 1 - msLobeS * (1 - msLobeW)
  bool  moonActive;          // any combined moon coefficient above threshold.

  // ---- Ray-constant (cloudShadeContextBeginRay) ----
  float sunCosTheta;         // dot(viewDirYUp, sunDirYUp)
  float sunFrac;             // sun-dot term of the sigma_ms remap
  float hg1;                 // forward lobe phase
  float hg2;                 // body lobe phase
  float powderMix;           // saturate(cloudPowderStrength) * view fade
  float moonCosTheta;        // dot(viewDirYUp, combinedMoonDir)
  float msMoonAniso;         // Wrenninge-smoothed moon phase sum
};

// Per-sample Nubis Cubed lighting result. `direct` is the sun in-scatter
// radiance per unit density × stepLen; caller multiplies during integration.
// `ambient` is the page-142 ambient term, same scaling convention.
struct NubisCubedLighting {
  vec3 direct;
  vec3 ambient;
};

// Nubis Cubed 2023 per-sample lighting evaluator CORE (pages 137, 142).
// Modeling inputs are parameters:
//   dim_profile — [0,1] outside->inside gradient. Legacy: the vertical-
//     profile proxy; Nubis3: the NVDF dimensional profile on its OWN depth
//     (nvdfLightingProfileDepthKm, fork 2026-09-08) — deliberately not the
//     density ramp, whose depth is set for solidity and, shared, pinned M at
//     1 and ambient_shape at 0 past the skin. See the sampler's step 7.
//   cloud_sdf   — signed distance in METERS, negative inside, expected
//     within [-cloudMsSdfDepth*4, 0]. Legacy: the slab/density proxy;
//     Nubis3: the live (coverage-offset) NVDF distance.
//   downTau     — macroscopic optical depth of the water OVERHEAD (km·sigma
//     units), for the underside light field. Legacy: height-LUT down-integral
//     × columnSpan × thickness × density; Nubis3: D_ambient grid tap × sigma.
//
// `dSunOverride` (fork — 2026-06-21, layer-2 echo-deck rework): >= 0 is used
// verbatim for the sun-direction optical depth (the deck passes an analytic
// proxy because the D_sun grid covers layer 1 only); negative samples the
// D_sun voxel grid — the production path.
NubisCubedLighting evalNubisCubedSampleCore(
    vec3 samplePos, float density,
    float dim_profile, float cloud_sdf, float downTau,
    float detailSigned,
    CloudShadeContext ctx,
    AtmosphereArgs args, bool isZUp, float dSunOverride) {

  float D_sun    = (dSunOverride >= 0.0f) ? dSunOverride
                                          : sampleDSun(samplePos, args, isZUp);
  float cosTheta = ctx.sunCosTheta;  // ray-constant (fork — 2026-07-30, perf)

  // === Direct scattering (paper p. 29 + p. 137) ===
  float sigma = args.cloudDensity;  // shared extinction coefficient
  float T_primary = exp(-sigma * D_sun);

  // sigma_ms remap (page 137 magic constants — exposed as RTX_OPTIONs).
  // sdfFrac: 0 when deep inside cloud, 1 at cloud surface.
  float sdfFrac = saturate((cloud_sdf + args.cloudMsSdfDepth) / max(args.cloudMsSdfDepth, 1e-4f));
  float sigma_ms_inner = lerp(args.cloudMsSigmaDeep, args.cloudMsSigmaShallow, sdfFrac);
  float sunFrac = ctx.sunFrac;  // ray-constant
  float sigma_ms = lerp(args.cloudMsSigmaShallow, sigma_ms_inner, sunFrac) * args.cloudMsScale;

  // === Billow micro-AO + powder (fork — 2026-07-14, detail-shading pass) ===
  //
  // Micro-AO: every other input here is km-scale smooth, so billows grown by
  // the erosion field were lit identically to the smooth cloud around them.
  // detailSigned is that same erosion field (zero-mean, positive = surface
  // grew outward here), reused as a local relief proxy: grown knuckles catch
  // more sky (brighten), carved crevices see less of it (darken). Gated by
  // sdfFrac so deep-core samples keep clean smooth shading. Applied to the
  // multi-scatter body lobe and the ambient terms; the primary forward lobe A
  // is exempt so backlit silver linings keep their pure Beer-Lambert read.
  float microAo = 1.0f;
  if (args.cloudMicroAoStrength > 0.0f) {
    // kMicroAoGain * cloudMicroAoStrength is frame-constant -> ctx.microAoScale.
    microAo = clamp(1.0f + ctx.microAoScale * sdfFrac * detailSigned, 0.15f, 1.25f);
  }

  // Powder (Schneider 2015): a low-density region viewed with the sun at the
  // viewer's back has few scatterers to redirect light back toward the eye,
  // so wisps and crevice walls go DARK against the bright dense body behind
  // them. 1 - exp(-k*density) saturates to ~1 inside the body, so the
  // darkening self-localizes to edges / crevices. Faded by view geometry so
  // forward-scatter silver linings are untouched.
  float powder = 1.0f;
  if (args.cloudPowderStrength > 0.0f) {
    const float kPowderSharpness = 4.0f;
    float powderTerm = 1.0f - exp(-kPowderSharpness * density);
    // saturate(strength) * (1 - smoothstep(-0.3, 0.5, cosTheta)) is ray-constant.
    powder = lerp(1.0f, powderTerm, ctx.powderMix);
  }

  // Underside light field (fork — realistic rework 2026-06-19). The diffuse
  // light reaching a point on the cloud's underside is the sky-dome light
  // that filtered DOWN through the water above it — downTau (param) is that
  // water's macroscopic optical depth. A Beer-Lambert term with a tunable
  // diffusion-flavored sigma (cloudUndersideLightSigma — downwelling flux is
  // multiply-scattered, so its effective extinction is far below the beam
  // sigma) turns it into a light factor: dark under thick cores, bright under
  // thin spots. The darkening strength fades as the sun nears the horizon so
  // low-sun light can rake UNDER the deck and produce lit sunset bases;
  // T_primary is exempt so backlit silver linings survive.
  //
  // darkenStrength is frame-constant (sun elevation only) and is exactly ZERO
  // across the whole night / low-sun band, where mix(1, skyDown, 0) == 1 — so
  // both the exp() and the D_ambient texture tap that produced downTau are dead
  // work there. The caller skips the tap on the same condition (fork —
  // 2026-07-30, perf); this branch keeps the evaluator honest either way.
  float verticalLight = 1.0f;
  if (ctx.darkenStrength > 0.0f) {
    float skyDown = exp(-args.cloudUndersideLightSigma * downTau);
    verticalLight = mix(1.0f, skyDown, ctx.darkenStrength);
  }

  float M = dim_profile * exp(-sigma_ms * D_sun) * verticalLight;
  float HG1 = ctx.hg1;  // hgPhaseNubis(cosTheta, cloudPhaseG1) — ray-constant
  float HG2 = ctx.hg2;  // hgPhaseNubis(cosTheta, cloudPhaseG2) — ray-constant

  // Energy-conserving dual-lobe blend (fork — 2026-06-19). Convex blend of
  // the single-scatter forward lobe A (silver lining) and the multi-scatter
  // body fill B, so the in-scatter phase integrates to exactly 1 and the
  // silver lining can't out-brighten the physical sky LUT it composites
  // against. cloudMsLobeWeight = w sets the forward/body balance;
  // cloudEnergyConserve = s lerps from the legacy additive sum (s=0,
  // byte-identical old look for an in-game A/B) to the conserving blend.
  float A = T_primary * HG1;
  // Micro-AO on the multi-scatter body lobe only: the body fill is the
  // diffuse-ish term where local relief shading reads as detail; the forward
  // lobe A stays pure so silver linings are unaffected.
  float B = M * HG2 * microAo;
  // Both convex-blend weights are frame-constant -> ctx.lobeWeightA / lobeWeightB.
  float L_direct = (A * ctx.lobeWeightA + B * ctx.lobeWeightB) * powder;

  // === Ambient scattering (paper p. 142) ===
  // Per-sample warm/cool ambient blend (fork — 2026-05-21). Warm color is
  // sky-view LUT sampled toward the sun; cool is the anti-sun horizon. The
  // blend signal shapes D_sun as 1 - exp(-D_sun * reach); the effect ramps
  // off above cloudSunsetAmbientRampHighSun so midday clouds are unchanged.
  //
  // sunsetFactor is frame-constant and is exactly 0 above cloudSunsetAmbientRampHighSun
  // (every midday frame), where coolMix collapses to 0 — so the exp() below is dead
  // work there. Gate on it (fork — 2026-07-30, perf); the multiply order is
  // unchanged so the blended result is bit-identical.
  vec3 skyRadiance = ctx.skyRadianceWarm;
  if (ctx.sunsetFactor > 0.0f) {
    float coolReachCurve = 1.0f - exp(-D_sun * args.cloudSunsetAmbientReachInvKm);
    float coolMix = saturate(coolReachCurve * ctx.sunsetFactor * args.cloudSunsetAmbientStrength);
    skyRadiance   = lerp(ctx.skyRadianceWarm, ctx.skyRadianceCool, coolMix);
  }

  // Ambient occlusion estimate: the analytic verticalLight REPLACES the
  // page-142 exp(-D_ambient) tap for the ambient shape (multiplying both
  // would double-count the same occlusion — see the 2026-06-19 rework note).
  float ambient_shape = pow(1.0f - dim_profile, 0.5f);

  // Ambient sun-shadowing (fork — 2026-07-14, dramatic-shading pass). The
  // sky-ambient fill dies off with the same optical depth that extinguishes
  // the direct lobes (Beer on D_sun with an internal diffusion-flavored
  // sigma), lerped in by the O(1) strength knob. Sunlit faces sit at
  // D_sun ~ 0 and keep full ambient; shaded cores plunge — the CoD4-style
  // contrast axis. The dome fill below is exempt by design (open-sky light
  // arriving from below/around the cloud, not through it).
  float ambientShadow = 1.0f;
  if (args.cloudAmbientShadowStrength > 0.0f) {
    const float kAmbientShadowSigma = 0.6f;  // per km of D_sun optical depth
    ambientShadow = mix(1.0f, exp(-kAmbientShadowSigma * D_sun), ctx.ambientShadowAmt);
  }

  // Top-down ambient: skylight that filtered DOWN through the overlying
  // cloud, colored by the warm/cool blend and attenuated by verticalLight.
  vec3 topAmbient = ambient_shape * verticalLight * skyRadiance * ambientShadow;

  // Sky-dome fill (fork — 2026-06-19, clouds-reflect-sky coupling). The
  // underside is ALSO lit by the open sky around and below the cloud, which
  // the water OVERHEAD does not block — deliberately bypasses verticalLight.
  // Reads the zenith sky color: bright daytime dome lifts undersides, dim
  // sunset zenith fades it naturally. cloudSkyAmbientFill scales; 0 = legacy.
  vec3 domeFill = ambient_shape * args.cloudSkyAmbientFill * ctx.skyRadianceZenith;

  NubisCubedLighting result;
  result.direct  = L_direct * ctx.sunRadiance;
  // Micro-AO on the ambient: sky light is the dominant fill on non-sun-facing
  // billows, so this is where the knuckle/crevice relief carries in most views.
  result.ambient = (topAmbient + domeFill) * microAo;
  return result;
}

// CloudShadeContext is declared ABOVE the Nubis Cubed evaluator (which now reads
// its hoisted per-frame / per-ray terms). buildCloudShadeContext fills the
// frame-constant half; cloudShadeContextBeginRay fills the view-direction half.
CloudShadeContext buildCloudShadeContext(AtmosphereArgs args) {
  // ===== Per-ray lighting source radiances =====
  // sunRadiance: args.sunIlluminance is raw user-tuned color (near-white in
  // FNV defaults) -- atmospheric extinction along the sun ray is NOT applied
  // CPU-side. We apply that `airTrans` extinction along the sun ray here so
  // the direct term picks up the warm-orange Rayleigh shift at low sun
  // elevation. Without this, result.direct stays white while result.ambient
  // (from the sky-view LUT) reads orange, producing a per-pixel hue mismatch
  // that varies with cloudPhaseG2 -- the bug the C6.4 diagnosis caught.
  //
  // skyRadiance: sampled from the sky-view LUT at the sun direction (clamped
  // to a hair above horizon so we don't return zero at exactly-on-horizon sun).
  // Deliberate deviation from page-142's "zenith ambient" -- zenith at sunset
  // is bluer than horizon, so sampling there would tint sunset cumulus bulk
  // blue. Sampling at the sun direction gives the actual warm-orange forward-
  // scatter atmosphere color matching the paper's pp. 122/132/171-173 renders.
  vec3 sunDirYUp = normalize(args.sunDirection);
  vec3 airTrans = getAtmosphericTransmittanceForDir(sunDirYUp, args);
  // Day/night gate on ALL of the cloud's daytime (sun-derived) lighting —
  // both the direct sun term and the sky-ambient terms (fork — issue #33,
  // extended for the night-brightness / retained-sunset-glow regression).
  //
  // Two things kept the clouds sunlit at night: (1) airTrans alone does not
  // fully extinguish the sun below the horizon, and (2) skyRadianceWarm/Cool
  // are sampled with the direction's y clamped just above the horizon (see the
  // max(..., 0.05) below), so the sky-view LUT keeps returning the warm horizon
  // (orange twilight) color no matter how far the sun has set — leaving clouds
  // glowing orange at -20deg elevation. The original #33 fix gated only the
  // direct term, which left that orange ambient as the sole night cloud light.
  //
  // Sunset-approach rolloff (fork — 2026-06-20). The upper bound was 0.02, which
  // held the cloud's daytime lighting at FULL brightness until the geometric
  // horizon and then crashed to night across [-0.05, 0.02]. That kept the sun +
  // sky-ambient terms at full strength through the entire low-sun band where
  // airTrans and the sky-view LUT are at their most saturated orange, so right
  // before sunset the clouds read as full-brightness, fully-saturated, flat
  // orange ("neon orange clouds" — worst below sun.y ~0.2, recovering once the
  // sun is well below the horizon). Widening the upper bound to 0.20 (~11.5deg)
  // dims the daytime cloud lighting PROGRESSIVELY as the sun descends through
  // that band (physically: the scene darkens through sunset), so the reddest
  // light is no longer the brightest. Above 0.20 daytime is unchanged (the good
  // "golden hour" window is untouched); at the horizon dayFactor ~= 0.2,
  // reaching 0 at -0.05 where the moon/night terms (nightFactor =
  // smoothstep(0.02, -0.05, sun.y)) take over. dayFactor and nightFactor are no
  // longer exact complements in [0.02, 0.20]; the small overlap is a gentle
  // pre-sunset dim, not a pop.
  float dayFactor = smoothstep(-0.05f, 0.20f, sunDirYUp.y);
  vec3 sunRadiance = args.sunIlluminance * airTrans * dayFactor;

  vec3 sunDirForAmbient = sunDirYUp;
  sunDirForAmbient.y = max(sunDirForAmbient.y, 0.05f);
  sunDirForAmbient = normalize(sunDirForAmbient);
  vec3 skyRadianceWarm = sampleSkyAmbientForVolume(sunDirForAmbient, args,
                                                    AtmosphereSkyViewLut,
                                                    AtmosphereCloudSkyTransmittanceLut,
                                                    AtmosphereSkyViewSampler) * dayFactor;

  // Anti-sun horizon sample for the per-sample warm/cool blend (fork —
  // 2026-05-21). Mirrors the sun-direction sample's small y-lift to keep
  // the lookup above sampleSkyAmbientForVolume's below-horizon zero-clamp.
  // At sunset, this picks up the deep teal / Belt-of-Venus pink band on the
  // opposite side of the sky; at midday, the cloudSunsetAmbientRampHighSun
  // smoothstep inside evalNubisCubedSampleCore rolls the contribution to zero
  // so this sample never reaches the composite.
  vec3 antiSunDir = vec3(-sunDirYUp.x, 0.05f, -sunDirYUp.z);
  antiSunDir = normalize(antiSunDir);
  // Same dayFactor gate as the warm sample (issue #33): fades the daytime
  // sky-ambient out at night so it can't keep tinting clouds below the horizon.
  vec3 skyRadianceCool = sampleSkyAmbientForVolume(antiSunDir, args,
                                                    AtmosphereSkyViewLut,
                                                    AtmosphereCloudSkyTransmittanceLut,
                                                    AtmosphereSkyViewSampler) * dayFactor;

  // Overhead (zenith) sky sample for the sky-dome underside fill (fork —
  // 2026-06-19, clouds-reflect-sky). This is the bright daytime sky that lights
  // cloud bottoms from below/around — the term the warm/cool sun-direction
  // samples above deliberately omit. Dim at sunset, so it self-fades and lets
  // the warm samples carry the look. Same dayFactor gate as the others.
  vec3 skyRadianceZenith = sampleSkyAmbientForVolume(vec3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f), args,
                                                    AtmosphereSkyViewLut,
                                                    AtmosphereCloudSkyTransmittanceLut,
                                                    AtmosphereSkyViewSampler) * dayFactor;

  // ===== Per-pixel moon-lighting precompute =====
  // The Nubis Cubed sun equations don't model moonlight, so without this block
  // the cloud RT renders black at night. Three contributions:
  //   - nightLight (ambient, per-ray): per-moon airglow fill + nightSkyColor.
  //     Added uncolored at composite time (atmospheric airglow, not cloud-
  //     scatter, so it sits outside cloudColor's parens).
  //   - moonDiffuseCoeff[mi] / moonPhaseAmt[mi] (directional, per-ray): per-moon
  //     Lambert and HG-phase-gain response gated by phaseGlow / nightFactor /
  //     cloudMoonBrightness, scaled by computeMoonSharedFactor — which ALREADY
  //     folds in atmospheric extinction `getAtmosphericTransmittanceForDir(
  //     m.direction, args)` for the moon ray, mirroring the airTrans treatment
  //     used for the sun. Split so the phase term can be Wrenninge-smoothed
  //     across HG-anisotropy octaves in the per-sample loop.
  //   - combinedMoonDir / combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff / combinedMoonPhaseAmt
  //     (Lever-4 approximation): one brightness-weighted shadow march along the
  //     combined direction replaces N independent per-moon marches.
  //
  // Smooth twilight transition: nightFactor = smoothstep(0.02, -0.05, sun.y)
  // ramps from 0 (sun above horizon) to 1 (sun ~3° below horizon), giving a
  // pop-free day -> night cross-fade on the cloud RT.
  vec3 nightLight = vec3(0.0f);
  vec3 moonDiffuseCoeff[MAX_MOONS];
  vec3 moonPhaseAmt[MAX_MOONS];
  vec3 moonDirNorm[MAX_MOONS];
  [unroll] for (uint mInit = 0u; mInit < MAX_MOONS; ++mInit) {
    moonDiffuseCoeff[mInit] = vec3(0.0f);
    moonPhaseAmt[mInit]     = vec3(0.0f);
    moonDirNorm[mInit]      = vec3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f);
  }
  {
    float sunElev = sunDirYUp.y;
    float nightFactor = smoothstep(0.02f, -0.05f, sunElev);
    if (nightFactor > 0.001f) {
      // [unroll] is load-bearing, not cosmetic (fork — 2026-07-30, perf). Both
      // sibling MAX_MOONS loops above and below are unrolled; this one was not,
      // and the dynamic `mi` index made Slang materialize the only local-memory
      // arrays in the whole shader — a 256-byte copy of the moons[] CB array
      // plus three vec3[4], all dynamic-index-only, i.e. the classic pattern
      // lowered to scratch. MAX_MOONS is a literal 4u, so unrolling makes every
      // index constant, SROAs all four arrays away, and removes the per-thread
      // CB->scratch copy. Values are bit-identical.
      [unroll] for (uint mi = 0u; mi < MAX_MOONS; ++mi) {
        MoonParams m = args.moons[mi];
        if (m.enabled < 0.5f) continue;
        vec3 moonDir = normalize(m.direction);
        float moonDot = max(dot(moonDir, vec3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f)), 0.0f);
        // phaseGlow peaks at full moon (phase=0.5), zero at new (0 or 1).
        float phaseGlow = 0.5f - 0.5f * cos(m.phase * 2.0f * kPi);

        // Ambient airglow contribution (uniform across the cloud volume).
        // Rebase (2026-06-14): the 0.0015 night-radiance scale moved into the
        // internal kMoonAirglowScale constant so the user-facing knob is an
        // O(1) "multiple of the calibrated night level" (default 1.0) instead
        // of a sub-0.01 raw gain. Effective value at the default is unchanged.
        const float kMoonAirglowScale = 0.0015f;
        vec3 sharedFactorAmbient = computeMoonSharedFactor(m, args, m.color);
        nightLight += sharedFactorAmbient * args.moonAmbientAirglow * kMoonAirglowScale
                    * moonDot * phaseGlow * args.cloudMoonBrightness * nightFactor;

        // Directional contribution — only above-horizon moons matter.
        if (moonDir.y > 0.01f && args.moonNeeStrength > 0.001f) {
          // Silver-lining response decomposed: diffuse (Lambert) amount and
          // phase (HG-gain) amount are precomputed here; the HG phase function
          // itself is evaluated per-sample in the march loop below so it can
          // be Wrenninge-smoothed across multiple anisotropy octaves.
          const float kMoonDiffuseGain = args.moonCloudDiffuseGain;
          const float kMoonPhaseGain   = args.moonCloudPhaseGain;
          float moonLambert = saturate(moonDir.y * 0.8f + 0.2f);

          // Physical irradiance scaffold — sharedFactor × phaseGlow × solidAngle
          // × moonNeeStrength × cloudMoonBrightness × nightFactor.
          vec3 sharedFactor = computeMoonSharedFactor(m, args, m.color);
          float moonSolidAngleSr = 2.0f * kPi * (1.0f - cos(m.angularRadius));
          vec3 moonIrradiance = sharedFactor * phaseGlow * moonSolidAngleSr
                              * args.moonNeeStrength * args.cloudMoonBrightness
                              * nightFactor;
          moonDiffuseCoeff[mi] = moonIrradiance * kMoonDiffuseGain * moonLambert;
          moonPhaseAmt[mi]     = moonIrradiance * kMoonPhaseGain;
          moonDirNorm[mi]      = moonDir;
        }
      }
      // Night-sky airglow baseline (uncolored, mirrors legacy `* 0.5f`).
      nightLight += args.nightSkyColor * args.nightSkyBrightness * nightFactor * 0.5f;

      // Starlight ambient coupling (fork).
      // Adds a faint per-ray ambient lift to cloud body lighting derived from
      // the sky's star contribution so cumulus under starry skies doesn't read
      // as pitch-black against HDR ~8 stars overhead.
      // Rebase (2026-06-14): kStarCloudCoupling absorbs the night-radiance
      // scale (and the calibrated reduction from the too-bright 0.03 raw gain)
      // so starAmbientCouplingStrength is an O(1) "multiple of the calibrated
      // night level" (default 1.0). This was the single largest UNIFORM night
      // cloud term (added uncolored to every sample), so it drove most of the
      // "clouds glow blue at night" read; coupled to args.starBrightness so the
      // lift tracks the user's star slider.
      const float kStarCloudCoupling = 0.008f;
      nightLight += args.nightSkyColor * args.starBrightness
                  * args.starAmbientCouplingStrength * kStarCloudCoupling * nightFactor;
    }
  }

  // Combined-moon shadow direction (Lever 4 — brightness-weighted average of
  // enabled, above-horizon moons). ONE shadow march per dense sample replaces
  // N independent marches; visual delta is near-zero because shadow direction
  // is a perceptual cue rather than a per-moon resolvable feature.
  vec3 combinedMoonDir          = vec3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f);
  vec3 combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff = vec3(0.0f);
  vec3 combinedMoonPhaseAmt     = vec3(0.0f);
  {
    vec3  sumWeightedDir = vec3(0.0f);
    float sumWeight      = 0.0f;
    [unroll] for (uint mi = 0u; mi < MAX_MOONS; ++mi) {
      // Weight by max-channel of total (diffuse + phase amplitude) — both
      // share moonIrradiance so this is proportional to per-moon brightness.
      vec3 moonTotal = moonDiffuseCoeff[mi] + moonPhaseAmt[mi];
      float w = max(max(moonTotal.r, moonTotal.g), moonTotal.b);
      if (w > 0.001f) {
        sumWeightedDir           += moonDirNorm[mi] * w;
        sumWeight                += w;
        combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff += moonDiffuseCoeff[mi];
        combinedMoonPhaseAmt     += moonPhaseAmt[mi];
      }
    }
    if (sumWeight > 0.0f && length(sumWeightedDir) > 0.001f) {
      combinedMoonDir = normalize(sumWeightedDir);
    }
  }

  // Direct-extinction sigma for the moon-shadow Beer-Lambert exponent. Matches
  // the legacy `baseSigma = 2 * cloudDensity`. The moon stays at single-octave
  // Beer-Lambert (no per-octave extinction reduction); the per-sample loop
  // applies Wrenninge phase smoothing only, to fix the visible ring artifact
  // from the sharp HG peak without lifting cumulus-interior brightness.
  // Note: deliberately NOT the Nubis Cubed `sigma = cloudDensity` — the moon
  // path stays on its own extinction scale to preserve the legacy look.
  float moonBaseSigma = 2.0f * args.cloudDensity;

  CloudShadeContext ctx;
  ctx.sunDirYUp                = sunDirYUp;
  ctx.sunRadiance              = sunRadiance;
  ctx.skyRadianceWarm          = skyRadianceWarm;
  ctx.skyRadianceCool          = skyRadianceCool;
  ctx.skyRadianceZenith        = skyRadianceZenith;
  ctx.combinedMoonDir          = combinedMoonDir;
  ctx.combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff = combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff;
  ctx.combinedMoonPhaseAmt     = combinedMoonPhaseAmt;
  ctx.nightLight               = nightLight;
  ctx.moonBaseSigma            = moonBaseSigma;
  ctx.msOctaveCount            = clamp(args.cloudMultiScatterOctaves, 1u, 4u);

  // ---- Frame-constant hoists out of the per-sample evaluator (fork —
  // 2026-07-30, cloud march perf). Each is the verbatim subexpression it
  // replaces, so the shaded result is unchanged. ----
  // One-planet geometry (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage 1): both frames the
  // march needs, computed once per frame instead of re-derived per call site.
  ctx.planetCenterKm      = getCloudPlanetCenter(args);
  ctx.planetCenterWorldKm = getCloudPlanetCenterWorldKm(args);
  ctx.planetRadiusKm      = args.planetRadius;

  const float kUndersideSunFadeElev = 0.35f;
  const float kMicroAoGain          = 1.5f;  // detailSigned excursion (~±0.3) -> shading units
  ctx.darkenStrength   = saturate(args.cloudBottomDarkening)
                       * smoothstep(0.0f, kUndersideSunFadeElev, saturate(sunDirYUp.y));
  ctx.microAoScale     = kMicroAoGain * args.cloudMicroAoStrength;
  ctx.ambientShadowAmt = saturate(args.cloudAmbientShadowStrength);
  ctx.sunsetFactor     = 1.0f - smoothstep(0.0f, args.cloudSunsetAmbientRampHighSun, sunDirYUp.y);

  float msLobeS   = saturate(args.cloudEnergyConserve);
  float msLobeW   = saturate(args.cloudMsLobeWeight);
  ctx.lobeWeightA = 1.0f - msLobeS * msLobeW;
  ctx.lobeWeightB = 1.0f - msLobeS * (1.0f - msLobeW);

  ctx.moonActive = any((combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff + combinedMoonPhaseAmt) > vec3(0.001f));

  // Ray-constant fields stay zeroed until cloudShadeContextBeginRay runs.
  ctx.sunCosTheta  = 0.0f;
  ctx.sunFrac      = 0.0f;
  ctx.hg1          = 0.0f;
  ctx.hg2          = 0.0f;
  ctx.powderMix    = 0.0f;
  ctx.moonCosTheta = 0.0f;
  ctx.msMoonAniso  = 0.0f;
  return ctx;
}

// =============================================================================
// Per-RAY shading precompute (fork — 2026-07-30, cloud march perf).
//
// Everything here depends only on the args CB and the pixel's view direction,
// yet all of it used to be recomputed at every one of the march's 64-96 samples.
// The Wrenninge moon phase-octave sum is the extreme case: a 3-iteration loop
// over hgPhase() (a pow(x, 1.5) and a divide each) whose inputs are a fixed dot
// product and a CB constant. Called once per ray from marchCloudLayers.
//
// Every expression is an exact copy of the per-sample original — no
// reassociation — so the render is bit-identical.
// =============================================================================
CloudShadeContext cloudShadeContextBeginRay(CloudShadeContext ctx, vec3 viewDirYUp,
                                            AtmosphereArgs args) {
  ctx.sunCosTheta = dot(viewDirYUp, ctx.sunDirYUp);
  ctx.sunFrac     = saturate(ctx.sunCosTheta / max(args.cloudMsSunDotMax, 1e-4f));
  ctx.hg1         = hgPhaseNubis(ctx.sunCosTheta, args.cloudPhaseG1);
  ctx.hg2         = hgPhaseNubis(ctx.sunCosTheta, args.cloudPhaseG2);
  ctx.powderMix   = saturate(args.cloudPowderStrength)
                  * (1.0f - smoothstep(-0.3f, 0.5f, ctx.sunCosTheta));

  // Wrenninge phase-octave smoothing of the moon lobe — identical constants to
  // the per-sample loop this replaces (kMsA / kMsC = 0.5).
  const float kMsA = 0.5f;
  const float kMsC = 0.5f;
  ctx.moonCosTheta = dot(viewDirYUp, ctx.combinedMoonDir);
  float msMoonAniso = 0.0f;
  float octEnergyM  = 1.0f;
  float octPhaseGM  = args.moonCloudAnisotropy;
  for (uint oct = 0u; oct < ctx.msOctaveCount; ++oct) {
    msMoonAniso += octEnergyM * hgPhase(ctx.moonCosTheta, octPhaseGM);
    octEnergyM  *= kMsA;
    octPhaseGM  *= kMsC;
  }
  ctx.msMoonAniso = msMoonAniso;
  return ctx;
}

// =============================================================================
// Per-slab raymarch helper. Encapsulates everything that varies per cloud
// layer when the slide-1 two-layer system is enabled: slab intersection,
// per-step density sampling, lighting composite, and Beer-Lambert
// accumulation. The static-per-frame shading state (sun/moon precomputes,
// Wrenninge octave constants, etc.) lives in CloudShadeContext so the
// caller can build it once and reuse it across both layer marches.
//
// Layer 1 (primary cumulus deck) is always marched. Layer 2 (cirrus deck
// when cloudLayer2Enable is true) is marched second with its own slab
// params and a zeroed-out moon coefficient set — cirrus is thin enough
// that moon-lit silver lining contributes nothing observable, and skipping
// the moon-shadow tap inside layer 2 stays on the deck's own slab band.
//
// Front-to-back ordering matters for the composite — the caller marches
// the LOWER altitude slab first so its alpha properly occludes the higher
// slab behind it (cumulus in front of cirrus when looking up from ground).
//
// tMinClamp / tMaxClamp (fork — 2026-06-10): clamp the marched segment to
// camera distances [tMinClamp, tMaxClamp]. 0 disables the respective clamp;
// 0/0 reproduces the pre-extraction march bit-for-bit. Both current callers
// pass 0/0 — the clamps support split-march experiments (two clamped
// segments partition the slab exactly, with no double-counted extinction).
#if CLOUD_MARCH_LIGHTNING
// In-cloud lightning flash glow (fork — 2026-07-14, tier 1). Radiance added to
// the in-scatter source at a march sample: inverse-square falloff about the
// strike position, softened over a ~0.5 km core (the strike is a channel, not
// a point), with an extinction-flavored exp standing in for the flash light's
// own attenuation through intervening cloud (no occlusion march — the cloud in
// front of the SAMPLE already attenuates via viewTransmittance). The caller's
// (1 - stepTransmittance) accumulation factor scales this by local density, so
// the glow lights cloud material only — a strike positioned where the column
// model placed no cloud simply lights nothing.
vec3 evalLightningFlash(vec3 samplePos, AtmosphereArgs args) {
  vec3 dv = samplePos - args.lightningStrikePosKm;
  float d2 = dot(dv, dv);
  const float kFlashCoreSoftKm2 = 0.25f;      // ~0.5 km channel-glow core
  const float kFlashExtinctionPerKm = 0.35f;  // in-cloud reach of the glow
  float fall = exp(-sqrt(d2) * kFlashExtinctionPerKm) / (d2 + kFlashCoreSoftKm2);
  return args.lightningColor * (args.lightningFlashIntensity * fall);
}
#endif

// =============================================================================
// Per-sample shading + Beer-Lambert accumulation shared by the fixed-step and
// √-adaptive march loops of marchCloudSlab (fork — detail round 2026-07-16).
// Verbatim extraction of the original loop body: evaluates the density model
// at camera distance tSampleKm along viewDirYUp, shades the sample (Nubis
// Cubed lighting + moon + optional lightning), and folds a Beer-Lambert
// segment of length segLenKm into accumColor / viewTransmittance. rayFrac
// positions the sample for the caller's hoisted entry/exit control-field
// lerp. Returns false when the sample was empty (density < 0.001 — nothing
// accumulated); sdfStepKmOut is the sampler's conservative empty-space radius
// in km (0 when the legacy sampler ran — it reports no SDF), valid either
// way, so callers can sphere-trace past provably-empty air.
bool integrateCloudSample(float tSampleKm,
                          float segLenKm,
                          float rayFrac,
                          vec3  viewDirYUp,
                          float slabAltitudeKm,
                          float slabThicknessKm,
                          float slabTypeMean,
                          float slabCoverageMean,
                          float slabDensityScale,
                          float typeNoiseEntry,
                          float typeNoiseExit,
                          float coverageNoiseEntry,
                          float coverageNoiseExit,
                          CloudShadeContext ctx,
                          AtmosphereArgs args,
                          inout vec3  accumColor,
                          inout float viewTransmittance,
                          out float sdfStepKmOut,
                          // Depth-companion accumulators (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud
                          // migration Stage 4a). Running sums for the transmittance-weighted mean
                          // cloud depth the screen pass writes to its depth companion RT; see the
                          // accumulation site below for the exact weight and marchCloudLayers for
                          // where the running sums are finalized into a mean. Unused (but still
                          // correctly accumulated) by callers that don't consume a depth output —
                          // cloud_secondary_lut.comp.slang passes throwaway locals.
                          inout float sumWeightedDistKm,
                          inout float sumWeight,
                          // Entry distance (fork -- 2026-09-06, occlusion accuracy). Nearest
                          // distance along this ray at which the march actually found cloud
                          // material, threaded through every caller so both layers narrow the
                          // same value. -1 on entry means "nothing found yet".
                          inout float firstDensityDistanceKm) {
  sdfStepKmOut = 0.0f;

  // World-anchored sample position (Y-up km). The slab intersection in the
  // caller runs with ray origin = vec3(0) and planet center = ctx.planetCenterKm
  // (getCloudPlanetCenter(args) — fork, world-space cloud migration Stage 1, 2026-09-05; was
  // (0, -cloudR, 0) under the retired cloud-only radius), so tSampleKm is measured along the view
  // ray from the camera. Adding
  // cameraWorldPosYUpKm anchors the sample's WORLD position to the player's
  // km-scale Y-up coordinates — the noise/coverage/type fields then translate
  // with the world rather than with the camera, giving the parallax depth cue
  // that was missing (clouds were reading as cardboard cutouts moving rigidly
  // with the player). Height-fraction math and slab geometry stay correct
  // because cameraWorldPosYUpKm magnitudes are small at FNV scale (~tens
  // of meters Y, sub-km XZ at sceneScale=2) compared to cloudThickness
  // (~3 km) and planet radius (~952 km curvature-adjusted), so the
  // length()-based altitude reading shifts negligibly. Voxel grids
  // (D_sun / D_ambient) are world-anchored on the bake side, so this also
  // brings the runtime lookup into alignment with the bake's convention.
  vec3 samplePos = args.cameraWorldPosYUpKm + viewDirYUp * tSampleKm;

  // Spatial coverage / type fields: per-sample lerp between the slab entry
  // and exit evaluations hoisted out of the march loop by the caller.
  // Variation amplitude (spread) comes from args — kept shared across layers
  // for v1 so layer 2 doesn't need its own spread knobs. Mean values are
  // per-slab so layer 2 can pick a different baseline (cirrus typeMean
  // ~= 0.05 vs cumulus 0.5).
  float typeNoise     = mix(typeNoiseEntry,     typeNoiseExit,     rayFrac);
  float coverageNoise = mix(coverageNoiseEntry, coverageNoiseExit, rayFrac);
  float typeLocal     = saturate(slabTypeMean     + (typeNoise     - 0.5f) * args.cloudTypeSpread);
  float coverageLocal = saturate(slabCoverageMean + (coverageNoise - 0.5f) * args.cloudCoverageSpread);

  float hf = computeCloudHeightFractionC(samplePos, ctx.planetCenterWorldKm, ctx.planetRadiusKm,
                                         slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm);
  float detailSigned;
  // Nubis3 model outputs: the real NVDF dimensional profile and the LIVE
  // (coverage-offset) signed distance. tSampleKm doubles as the camera
  // distance for the near-camera HF detail blend (the slab intersection
  // runs with ray origin = the camera).
  float nubisProfile;
  float nubisSdfKm;
  float density = sampleCloudDensityNubis3(samplePos, hf, typeLocal, coverageLocal,
                                           slabDensityScale, tSampleKm,
                                           args.cloudDetailLodEnable != 0u ? segLenKm : 0.0f, args,
                                           AtmosphereCloudNvdfSdf, AtmosphereCloudDetailNoise3D,
                                           AtmosphereCloudNoise3DSampler,
                                           /*coarseOd=*/false,
                                           nubisProfile, detailSigned, nubisSdfKm,
                                           sdfStepKmOut);
  if (density < 0.001f) {
    return false;
  }

  // First-density-hit entry (fork -- 2026-09-06, occlusion accuracy). Reuses the empty-sample
  // gate immediately above as the definition of "cloud starts here", so this introduces no new
  // threshold: whatever the march already counts as material is what sets the entry distance.
  // Narrowed with a min rather than a plain first-write because the two layers share one value
  // and either may hold the nearer cloud along a given ray.
  if (firstDensityDistanceKm < 0.0f || tSampleKm < firstDensityDistanceKm) {
    firstDensityDistanceKm = tSampleKm;
  }

  // ===== Contribution-weighted lighting LOD (fork — 2026-07-30, perf) =====
  //
  // The Beer-Lambert and aerial-perspective factors are computed HERE rather
  // than at the accumulation below. This is an exact reorder — they depend only
  // on density, segLenKm and tSampleKm, all of which are final at this point —
  // and it makes the sample's true contribution weight available BEFORE the
  // expensive lighting runs.
  //
  // sampleWeight is precisely the factor this sample's color is multiplied by
  // when it lands in accumColor (see the accumulation at the end of this
  // function), so it is the exact measure of how much the pixel can possibly
  // care about getting this sample's lighting right.
  //
  // Why this matters: the near-field sun refinement costs TWO full
  // density-sampler calls (~9 of the ~16 texture taps per dense sample) and was
  // gated on `density >= 0.05` alone — nothing about visibility. In an optically
  // thick view viewTransmittance decays geometrically (sigma = cloudDensity = 4
  // at defaults), so a large fraction of dense samples sit deep in the cloud
  // contributing almost nothing to the pixel while paying full price. Measured:
  // dragging nubis3SunNearFieldKm 3 -> 0 is worth ~1 ms of the 3.3 ms pass.
  //
  // Including aerialT_haze in the weight makes distance-dimmed far samples fall
  // out automatically, with an error bound, rather than needing a hand-tuned
  // distance cutoff.
  //
  // Both fallbacks are the ones the existing density gates already use: the sun
  // path drops to the D_sun grid (dSunOverride stays -1) and the moon path to
  // unshadowed moonlight — so this only ever coarsens lighting that was already
  // designed to degrade that way, never removes cloud material.
  //
  // No temporal state, no held value, no refresh quantum: sampleWeight is a
  // deterministic function of already-jittered quantities, so the switch point
  // moves with the jitter like every other threshold in the march. This is
  // categorically unlike the posterization family.
  //
  // Threshold 0 disables the LOD entirely (weight >= 0 is always true).
  float effectiveDensity   = density * args.cloudDensity;
  float stepTransmittance  = exp(-effectiveDensity * segLenKm);
  float aerialT_haze       = exp(-args.cloudAerialHazePerKm * tSampleKm);
  float aerialT_fade       = exp(-args.cloudAerialFadePerKm * tSampleKm);
  float sampleWeight       = viewTransmittance * aerialT_haze * (1.0f - stepTransmittance);
#if !CLOUD_PROFILE_DENSITY_ONLY
  bool  refineLighting     = sampleWeight >= args.cloudLightingLodThreshold;

  // Nubis Cubed lighting at this sample. samplePos is already Y-up, so
  // isZUp = false for the D_sun / D_ambient lookups. The evaluator core gets
  // the REAL modeling inputs — the NVDF dimensional profile, the live SDF in
  // meters (feeds the sigma_ms remap + micro-AO gate), and a D_ambient grid
  // tap × sigma as the water-overhead optical depth for the underside light
  // field.
  //
  // NOTE: the D_sun / D_ambient voxel grids are baked over the primary
  // (layer 1) slab only — layer 2 samples will read whichever residual
  // density the bake covered. For cirrus this is acceptable; the grids
  // contribute attenuation toward zenith and sun direction at most, and
  // cirrus is too thin to need precomputed shadow data of its own.
  float cloudSdfM = clamp(nubisSdfKm * 1000.0f, -args.cloudMsSdfDepth * 4.0f, 0.0f);
  // D_ambient tap skipped when the underside-light darkening is off (fork —
  // 2026-07-30, perf): downTau's ONLY consumer is verticalLight, which is
  // exactly 1 whenever ctx.darkenStrength is 0 — the entire night / low-sun
  // band, and any config with cloudBottomDarkening 0. One texture tap plus an
  // exp() per dense sample, free.
  float downTau = 0.0f;
  if (ctx.darkenStrength > 0.0f) {
    downTau = sampleDAmbient(samplePos, args, /*isZUp=*/false) * args.cloudDensity;
  }

  // Sun occlusion comes entirely from the D_sun voxel grid (fork — 2026-07-30).
  //
  // This is where the near-field LIVE sun refinement used to run: two extra
  // density-sampler calls per dense lit sample, marching the first 3 km toward
  // the sun and handing off to the grid beyond. It existed because the grid was
  // believed to low-pass lobe-scale self-shadowing away. It did not — the bake
  // already spends 5-8 taps inside that same 3 km (denser along the sun ray than
  // the refinement's 2), and grid texels sit far above Nyquist for 1-2.5 km
  // lobes. The actual defect was a FROZEN per-voxel tap phase, whose noise
  // through exp(-sigma * OD) buried the lobe gradients under static mottle.
  //
  // De-jittering the bake's near section (a deterministic fixed-count quadrature
  // — see sampleCloudSunOpticalDepthAtWorld) fixed the estimator instead of
  // papering over it per sample. The grid-only result was preferred over the old
  // live-refined look in an 11-person vote, on top of recovering ~1 ms of the
  // 3.3 ms pass, so the live path is gone rather than merely defaulted off.
  //
  // Note for anyone re-introducing per-sample sun work here: this pass is
  // tap-latency-bound and warp-synchronized, so per-lane conditional work does
  // NOT convert to time — a contribution-weighted gate on exactly this block
  // measured a recovery of precisely zero. Only warp/dispatch-uniform reductions
  // (or moving work to the once-per-frame bake, as here) pay off.
  float dSunOverride = -1.0f;

  NubisCubedLighting lighting = evalNubisCubedSampleCore(
      samplePos, density, nubisProfile, cloudSdfM, downTau, detailSigned,
      ctx, args, /*isZUp=*/false, dSunOverride);

  // ===== Per-sample moon direct light =====
  // Same Beer-Lambert single-octave moon shadow march as before. The if-gate
  // automatically skips the entire block when combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff +
  // combinedMoonPhaseAmt are zero — which is exactly how the caller suppresses
  // the moon path for layer 2 (cirrus) without an extra flag parameter.
  const float kMoonShadowSkipDensity = 0.05f;
  vec3 moonDirectLight = vec3(0.0f);
  if (ctx.moonActive) {
    float moonOpticalDepth = 0.0f;
#if !CLOUD_PROFILE_NO_MOON_SHADOWS
    if (density >= kMoonShadowSkipDensity && refineLighting) {
      moonOpticalDepth = sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth_local(
          samplePos, ctx.combinedMoonDir, typeLocal, coverageLocal,
          ctx.planetCenterWorldKm, ctx.planetRadiusKm, args);
    }
#endif
    float moonShadow = exp(-moonOpticalDepth * ctx.moonBaseSigma);
    // msMoonAniso (the Wrenninge phase-octave sum) is ray-constant — precomputed
    // in cloudShadeContextBeginRay instead of per sample (fork — 2026-07-30, perf).
    moonDirectLight = (ctx.combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff
                     + ctx.combinedMoonPhaseAmt * ctx.msMoonAniso) * moonShadow;
  }

  // Composite: cloudColor multiplies all surface-scatter terms; nightLight
  // is added uncolored, identical to the legacy single-layer march.
  //
  // Thin-sample ambient fade (fork — 2026-06-13). The coverage gate leaves a
  // soft sub-threshold skirt around every silhouette; those thin samples are
  // ambient-dominated, and the ambient is sampled at the horizon (a dirty
  // grey-brown), so the skirt reads as a discolored haze halo against clear
  // sky. Weight the ambient by the sample's own (gated) density so the
  // faintest edge samples fall toward transparent instead of horizon-tinted.
  // Direct / moon / night terms are untouched, so backlit edges keep their glow.
  // Driven live by cloudEdgeAmbientFade; 0 disables the fade (full ambient).
  float ambientThinFade = args.cloudEdgeAmbientFade > 1e-5f
                        ? smoothstep(0.0f, args.cloudEdgeAmbientFade, density)
                        : 1.0f;
  vec3 sampleColor = args.cloudColor
                   * (lighting.direct + lighting.ambient * ambientThinFade + moonDirectLight)
                   + ctx.nightLight;

#if CLOUD_MARCH_LIGHTNING
  // Lightning flash glow (fork — 2026-07-14): view pass only, inert unless a
  // strike envelope is live this frame.
  if (args.lightningFlashIntensity > 0.0f) {
    sampleColor += evalLightningFlash(samplePos, args);
  }
#endif

#else
  vec3 sampleColor = vec3(1.0f);
#endif

  // Beer-Lambert step accumulation. effectiveDensity / stepTransmittance and the
  // two aerial factors were computed above, before the lighting, so the
  // contribution-weighted LOD could use them (exact reorder — same values).
  vec3 stepRadiance = sampleColor * (1.0f - stepTransmittance);

  // Aerial perspective split into two independent effects so cloud
  // appearance close to camera can be tuned separately from horizon
  // wall prevention:
  //   - aerialT_haze: dims cloud RADIANCE with distance (visual softness)
  //   - aerialT_fade: softens cloud EXTINCTION accumulation with distance
  //                   so horizon-grazing rays don't pile up into a wall
  // At default 0.05/0.05 both effects are mild; setting either to 0
  // disables that half of the aerial perspective independently.
  // tSampleKm is true camera distance in EVERY consumer of this helper (all
  // keep the camera-at-origin convention), so haze/fade behave identically
  // across passes.
  accumColor += stepRadiance * viewTransmittance * aerialT_haze;
  // Depth-companion accumulation (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage 4a):
  // sampleWeight (computed above for the lighting LOD gate) is EXACTLY the coefficient this
  // sample's color is weighted by in the accumColor update just above -- viewTransmittance has not
  // been mutated between the two -- so reusing it here gives a true transmittance-weighted mean
  // distance at zero extra cost. marchCloudLayers divides sumWeightedDistKm / sumWeight once, after
  // both layers have contributed.
  sumWeightedDistKm += tSampleKm * sampleWeight;
  sumWeight         += sampleWeight;
  viewTransmittance *= mix(1.0f, stepTransmittance, aerialT_fade);
  return true;
}

// =============================================================================
// Ray-vs-cloud-slab span in the camera-relative Y-up frame (fork - 2026-09-05, world-space cloud
// migration Stage 1; ported verbatim from archaeology commit 30d20a8f5).
//
// Returns the marchable [tEntry, tExit] segment of the spherical annulus between the slab's base
// and top shells for an eye at ANY radius, then clips it against the planet.
//
// What this replaces (the far-root-only selection formerly inline in marchCloudSlab and
// marchEchoDeck) took the far root of each shell unconditionally: tEntry = t1base, tExit = t1top.
// That is correct only while the eye is below the deck, which was the only case the old frame could
// express, because the camera sat welded to the surface at altitude 0 (getEyeRadius(args) ==
// args.planetRadius exactly — still true THIS stage; see that function's comment). With a real
// cameraAltitudeKm (Stage 2) the eye can also be inside the slab or above it, so the span is now the
// honest set difference [t0top, t1top] minus (t0base, t1base). Below the deck it reduces to exactly
// [t1base, t1top] - the legacy result, unchanged. This is why the correctness bar for Stage 1 is "at
// planetRadius=952, near-identical to before": the far-root branch below IS the legacy path, reached
// unconditionally while the eye's radius equals getEyeRadius(args) (i.e. always, until Stage 2).
//
// The planet clip is what replaces cloud_render's viewDirYUp.y <= 0.001 early-out (removed this
// stage). That test cut the deck off at eye level with a knife, and that cut IS the hard horizon
// line the sky used to end on: the deck's far edge asymptotes to y == 0, so slicing at y == 0 slices
// exactly along it. The real terminator is the ground. A below-horizon ray now marches the deck's
// UNDERSIDE receding away from the eye and stops where it strikes the planet, so the deck runs down
// to the true horizon and ends there because something is genuinely in the way. A ray passing
// between the horizon and the tangent misses the planet and keeps going, which is also correct -
// that is the deck still visible beyond the horizon.
//
// Piece-2 marching (rendering the deck's far underside when looking down through a gap in the near
// side) is explicitly NOT this function's job yet — Stage 5 owns it; this ports the reference's
// single-piece selection exactly as it stands, matching that stage's stated deferral.
// spanPiece selects which of the up-to-two slab crossings along this ray to return (fork --
// 2026-09-06, fly-through polish). Piece 0 is the near crossing, piece 1 the far one, and piece 1
// exists only when the eye is ABOVE the deck base looking down through it: the ray then crosses the
// shell twice, once on the way down and once on the far side of the planet-facing gap.
//
// Only piece 0 was ever marched, which cost two things. The deck's far underside simply did not
// render when looking down through a gap -- the geometry was there, nothing sampled it. And the
// span was discontinuous in eye radius: descending through the base shell switched from "near
// crossing only" to "far crossing only" with nothing bridging them, which is the branch
// discontinuity the old dead-zone ring came from. Marching both pieces closes the gap from both
// sides -- as the eye sinks to the base, piece 0 shrinks to nothing while piece 1 becomes exactly
// the span the below-base branch returns.
bool cloudSlabSpan(vec3 rayDirYUp, vec3 planetCenter, float planetRadiusKm,
                   float slabAltitudeKm, float slabThicknessKm, int spanPiece,
                   out float tEntry, out float tExit) {
  tEntry = 0.0f;
  tExit  = -1.0f;

  const float baseRadius = planetRadiusKm + slabAltitudeKm;
  const float topRadius  = planetRadiusKm + slabAltitudeKm + slabThicknessKm;

  float t0top, t1top;
  if (!intersectSphere(vec3(0.0f), rayDirYUp, planetCenter, topRadius, t0top, t1top)) return false;
  if (t1top <= 0.0f) return false;

  float t0base, t1base;
  const bool hitsBase = intersectSphere(vec3(0.0f), rayDirYUp, planetCenter, baseRadius,
                                        t0base, t1base);

  if (!hitsBase || t1base <= 0.0f) {
    // Base shell missed entirely, or wholly behind us: the full top-shell chord is slab, and there
    // is no second crossing.
    if (spanPiece != 0) return false;
    tEntry = max(t0top, 0.0f);
    tExit  = t1top;
  } else if (t0base > 0.0f) {
    // Base shell ahead of the eye, so the eye is outside/above it and the ray crosses the shell
    // twice: down through the near side, then out through the far side.
    if (spanPiece == 0) {
      tEntry = max(t0top, 0.0f);
      tExit  = t0base;
    } else {
      // The far crossing -- the deck's underside seen through a gap. This is the piece that used
      // not to be marched at all.
      tEntry = max(t1base, 0.0f);
      tExit  = t1top;
    }
  } else {
    // Eye at or below the base shell - one crossing only, the legacy ground-level path.
    if (spanPiece != 0) return false;
    tEntry = max(t1base, 0.0f);
    tExit  = t1top;
  }

  float tPlanet0, tPlanet1;
  if (intersectSphere(vec3(0.0f), rayDirYUp, planetCenter, planetRadiusKm, tPlanet0, tPlanet1)
      && tPlanet0 > 0.0f) {
    tExit = min(tExit, tPlanet0);
  }

  return tExit > tEntry && tExit > 0.0f;
}

// Accumulates one march segment's attenuation into the transmittance measured up to surfaceKm
// (fork -- 2026-09-06, measured occlusion prefix).
//
// Why a prefix at all: the composite has to answer "how much of this ray's cloud is IN FRONT of the
// surface". Estimating that from (entry, mean, alpha) cannot work -- those three numbers do not say
// where along the ray the density sat, so a mountain with thin wisps in front of it and a dense bank
// behind gets the same answer as the reverse. That is the "clouds visible on the other side of
// geometry" artefact. The march already walks the density in order, so it can simply measure it.
//
// Transmittance is MULTIPLICATIVE, so this is order-independent: segments may be accumulated in any
// sequence and across both layers, which matters because layer 2 marches after layer 1 but can lie
// nearer along a downward ray. The segment straddling the surface is split analytically --
// pow(factor, frac) is exactly Beer's law over the partial length -- so the result does not
// quantise to the step size, which would otherwise dither along the contact line as the jitter
// moves the sample.
//
// A ray with no surface passes surfaceKm = kNoSurfaceKm, which makes every frac 1 and leaves the
// prefix equal to the full transmittance, i.e. full visibility, with no branch.
// Distance standing for "this ray hit nothing", far beyond any reachable cloud span. Passing it
// rather than branching leaves the prefix equal to the full transmittance, i.e. visibility 1.
static const float kNoSurfaceKm = 1e9f;

// Transmittance-limit exit for every view march loop (Nubis p.174) -- AND the scale the reported
// transmittance is expressed on (fork -- 2026-09-08, disc leak; see the rescale at the bottom of
// marchCloudLayers).
//
// The loops stop once viewTransmittance falls below this and composite what they have, so the
// background still shows through a cloud they already declared opaque, at up to 1% of its
// radiance. Against the sky (~1 radiance unit) that is invisible: ~0.01 added to a cloud. The sun
// is not a disc drawn anywhere in this renderer, though -- it is the Mie aureole the sky-view LUT
// bakes at mieAnisotropy 0.97 (hgPhase peaks at 174x isotropic), a blob of order 20-50 radiance
// units near the sun, brighter and redder the lower it sits. 1% of that is 0.2-0.5, some 4-10x the
// radiance of a dark overcast deck (~0.05), so it reads as a sun disc rendered ON TOP of cloud the
// march had already found opaque; the moon disc leaks the same way against the night deck. The
// composite honours the premultiplied (rgb, T) convention exactly -- the leak is the early-out,
// not the composite. Physically a 1.5 km deck at sigma 6.7 passes exp(-10) ~ 5e-5, not 1e-2.
//
// The cure is to report transmittance on the scale whose ZERO is this threshold (marchCloudLayers,
// once per ray). A ray that took the early exit was below the threshold by the test's own
// definition, so it reports exactly 0 and leaks nothing -- everywhere on screen, at every view
// angle, for one multiply-add per ray.
//
// Two rejected alternatives, both tried on 2026-09-08, and the reason the shift is the shape the
// fix has to take: what the eye sees is T x background, so ANY rule that removes the leak on one
// side of a boundary and keeps it on the other draws that boundary in the sky at up to 1% of an
// HDR background -- several times the cloud's own radiance.
//   * Tightening the threshold toward the sun (a smoothstep on the view-sun angle, 0.01 outside
//     ~16 degrees, 1e-4 inside ~8) drew a literal circle centred on the sun: a large, smooth,
//     near-black disc over dawn cloud, with the unaffected horizon-fade glow still burning at its
//     centre. Reported by the user. No cloud shape explains a circle in the sky, which is what
//     made it far worse than the leak it removed.
//   * Zeroing T at the break moves that same edge onto the T = threshold contour, which follows
//     cloud shape in general but, near a low sun, is a near-horizontal line at the elevation where
//     the aerial fade stops dominating -- a hard cut through the glow.
// The shift has no boundary at all: it is continuous and monotone in T, exact at T = 1, and
// reaches 0 exactly where the march stops being able to tell the difference.
//
// The honest alternative, marching every opaque ray down to 1e-4 instead, is ~12 more steps at the
// ~0.4 optical depths an interior step carries -- 15-20% on the pass, to refine pixels that are
// already opaque, by at most 1% of the background.
static const float kCloudExitTransmittance = 0.01f;

// Density-evaluation counter (fork -- 2026-09-17, native-scale optimisation). Per-thread static, so
// counting costs no signature changes anywhere in the march. The screen pass zeroes it before
// marchCloudLayers and writes the total into the depth companion's free .w channel, where
// DEBUG_VIEW_CLOUD_SAMPLE_COUNT renders it. Lighting is only ~17% of the march, so the march is
// dominated by density evaluation and the question that chooses every optimisation from here is
// where those evaluations actually go -- which rays spend their budget, and on what. Measure first.
static uint g_cloudMarchSamples;

void marchCloudSlab(vec3   viewDirYUp,
                    float  pixelJitter,
                    float  pixelJitterStatic,
                    float  slabAltitudeKm,
                    float  slabThicknessKm,
                    float  slabTypeMean,
                    float  slabCoverageMean,
                    float  slabDensityScale,
                    float  slabNoiseSeedOffset,
                    float  tMinClamp,
                    float  tMaxClamp,
                    // Which slab crossing to march; see cloudSlabSpan. Callers march piece 0, then
                    // piece 1, so accumulation stays front-to-back across both.
                    int    spanPiece,
                    uint   sampleCount,
                    CloudShadeContext ctx,
                    AtmosphereArgs args,
                    inout vec3  accumColor,
                    inout float viewTransmittance,
                    // Depth-companion outputs (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage
                    // 4a). entryDistanceKm is written once below, unconditionally, the moment this
                    // slab's span is known valid -- layer 1 (this function, always marched first,
                    // front-to-back) is "the marched span" the screen pass's depth companion means
                    // by "entry distance"; marchEchoDeck's layer-2 span does not touch it.
                    // sumWeightedDistKm / sumWeight are the same running accumulators
                    // integrateCloudSample updates, threaded through so both layers contribute to
                    // one combined mean-depth estimate.
                    inout float entryDistanceKm,
                    inout float sumWeightedDistKm,
                    inout float sumWeight) {
  // Slab intersection (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage 1). Was inline
  // ray-sphere tests against the base + top shells of the curvature-adjusted cloud planet, taking
  // the far root of each unconditionally; now cloudSlabSpan, which shares the one planet centre and
  // radius with the rest of the atmosphere and additionally clips against the planet (see its
  // comment for why — this is what lets cloud_render drop the below-horizon early-out).
  float tEntry, tExit;
  if (!cloudSlabSpan(viewDirYUp, ctx.planetCenterKm, ctx.planetRadiusKm,
                     slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm, spanPiece, tEntry, tExit)) return;

  tEntry = max(tEntry, tMinClamp);
  if (tMaxClamp > 0.0f) {
    tExit = min(tExit, tMaxClamp);
  }
  if (tEntry >= tExit || tExit <= 0.0f) return;
  tEntry = max(tEntry, 0.0f);

  // Entry distance (fork — 2026-09-05, Stage 4a): this WAS the geometric slab-entry
  // distance, in km, written regardless of whether any density was actually found along the ray --
  // "where the marched span started" as a property of the slab geometry, not the cloud coverage.
  //
  // SUPERSEDED (fork -- 2026-09-06, occlusion accuracy). `entryDistanceKm = tEntry` used to sit
  // here. Its consumer, cloudDepthVisibility in composite.comp.slang, ramps cloud occlusion up
  // from the entry distance, so reporting where the slab GEOMETRY starts fogs every surface past
  // that point even when the nearest actual cloud along the ray is kilometres further out --
  // which is the "clouds drawn over geometry they should be behind" artefact. The distance is now
  // narrowed to the first sample carrying real density, inside integrateCloudSample and inside
  // marchEchoDeck. A ray whose slab is empty leaves the caller's -1 sentinel untouched: the
  // honest answer, and the one loadCurrentCloudOpacity already reads as "no cloud on this pixel".

  // Adaptive step count (fork — 2026-06-12, adaptive march sampling). A
  // FIXED step count across a span that varies from ~4 km (zenith) to
  // 50+ km (horizon-grazing through the curved shell) means horizon rays
  // sample ~2 km cloud features with ~1.6 km steps — aliasing that the
  // jitter + temporal EMA render as soft horizontal BANDS, concentrated
  // exactly where the sky compresses toward the horizon. Hold a target
  // step LENGTH instead: count = span / cloudViewStepKm, floored at the
  // configured base count when the cap allows it, and capped at
  // cloudViewSamplesMax (perf bound; the column model's pre-tap early-outs
  // keep the added steps cheap where the slab is empty).
  // cloudViewStepKm = 0 reproduces the legacy fixed count exactly.
  uint effSampleCount = sampleCount;
  if (args.cloudViewStepKm > 0.001f) {
    uint wanted = uint(ceil((tExit - tEntry) / args.cloudViewStepKm));
    uint maxSamples = max(uint(args.cloudViewSamplesMax), 2u);
    effSampleCount = clamp(wanted, min(sampleCount, maxSamples), maxSamples);
  }

  float stepLen = (tExit - tEntry) / float(effSampleCount);

  // Coverage / type control fields hoisted out of the march loop (fork —
  // 2026-06-10, perf). The fields vary at ~300 km wavelength
  // (cloudCoverage/TypeNoiseScale ~ 0.0033 cycles/km) while a slab ray
  // spans at most ~100 km at the horizon, so evaluating the 9-hash Worley
  // field twice per STEP (64 evals/ray at 32 steps) was almost entirely
  // redundant. Evaluate at the ray's slab entry and exit, lerp per step —
  // exact at the endpoints; the worst-case interior deviation is a
  // smoothing of weather-region boundaries ALONG the ray, far below the
  // jitter noise floor. (The voxel-grid bake integrals already use the
  // same single-position reuse.)
  vec3 entryPosCF = args.cameraWorldPosYUpKm + viewDirYUp * tEntry;
  vec3 exitPosCF  = args.cameraWorldPosYUpKm + viewDirYUp * tExit;
  vec2 entryXZ = vec2(entryPosCF.x, entryPosCF.z);
  vec2 exitXZ  = vec2(exitPosCF.x,  exitPosCF.z);
  float typeNoiseEntry = 0.5f;
  float typeNoiseExit = 0.5f;
  float coverageNoiseEntry = 0.5f;
  float coverageNoiseExit = 0.5f;
  if (args.cloudTypeSpread != 0.0f) {
    typeNoiseEntry = cloudControlField2D(entryXZ * args.cloudTypeNoiseScale, 200.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
    typeNoiseExit = cloudControlField2D(exitXZ * args.cloudTypeNoiseScale, 200.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
  }
  if (args.cloudCoverageSpread != 0.0f) {
    coverageNoiseEntry = cloudControlField2D(entryXZ * args.cloudCoverageNoiseScale, 250.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
    coverageNoiseExit = cloudControlField2D(exitXZ * args.cloudCoverageNoiseScale, 250.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
  }

  // √-adaptive hybrid march gate (fork — detail round 2026-07-16). Active
  // with a nonzero adaptive step floor (the knob is the off-switch) and a
  // step-length target to key growth off.
  bool adaptiveMarch = args.nubis3AdaptiveStepKm > 1e-4f
                    && args.cloudViewStepKm > 0.001f;

  if (!adaptiveMarch) {
    // ---- Fixed-length lattice march (legacy stepping, bit-identical) ----
    for (uint i = 0u; i < effSampleCount; ++i) {
      if (viewTransmittance < kCloudExitTransmittance) break;  // early-exit (free perf win)

      float t = tEntry + stepLen * (float(i) + pixelJitter);
      float rayFrac = (float(i) + pixelJitter) / float(effSampleCount);
      float sdfStepKm;
      ++g_cloudMarchSamples;
      bool sampled = integrateCloudSample(t, stepLen, rayFrac, viewDirYUp,
                                          slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm,
                                          slabTypeMean, slabCoverageMean,
                                          slabDensityScale,
                                          typeNoiseEntry, typeNoiseExit,
                                          coverageNoiseEntry, coverageNoiseExit,
                                          ctx, args, accumColor, viewTransmittance,
                                          sdfStepKm, sumWeightedDistKm, sumWeight, entryDistanceKm);
      // SDF empty-space skip (fork — Nubis3 conversion Phase C). The sampler
      // reported a guaranteed-empty radius around this sample (min hex tap,
      // coverage- and wobble-conservative); jump the loop index so the next
      // sample lands just inside that radius. Advancing by whole indices
      // keeps every sample on the jittered stratified lattice — temporal
      // behavior and banding characteristics are unchanged, rays just stop
      // paying for steps that provably hit nothing. nvdfStepScale is the
      // safety factor on top (0 disables the skip entirely); the uint cap is
      // the usual CB-driven-loop hard-cap discipline.
      if (!sampled && args.nvdfStepScale > 0.0f) {
        float skipKm = sdfStepKm * args.nvdfStepScale;
        if (skipKm > stepLen) {
          uint advance = uint(min(floor(skipKm / stepLen), 4096.0f));
          i += advance - 1u;  // the loop's ++i completes the advance
        }
      }
    }
    return;
  }

  // ---- √-adaptive hybrid march (fork — detail round 2026-07-16; Nubis
  // p.172/174). A FIXED step length is the detail CEILING: 100 m steps
  // cannot resolve the sub-200 m content the detail volume now carries, no
  // matter how good the texture gets. The reference marches
  //   step = max(SDF distance, adaptive),  adaptive = sqrt(dist) · k
  // — sphere-tracing past empty air (the SDF half, subsuming the Phase C
  // index-skip above) and sampling finely near the camera, coarsening with
  // distance as the pixel footprint grows. Our mapping keeps the existing
  // knob's meaning at the noise-tile distance:
  //   adaptive(t) = clamp(cloudViewStepKm · sqrt(t / 12 km),
  //                       nubis3AdaptiveStepKm, 4 · cloudViewStepKm)
  // → 100 m at 12 km (unchanged by default), ~30 m at the 1.3 km deck base,
  // the floor (default 25 m) for fly-through, growing to ~400 m at the far
  // horizon. Sample positions are jittered inside each cell; the cursor stays unjittered.
  // Budget exhaustion is handled by the coarse tail integration below.
  const float kAdaptiveRefDistKm = 12.0f;  // adaptive == cloudViewStepKm here
  // Animated by default so sampling error does not become a fixed screen-space pattern.
  const float kJitterAnimateKm   = args.nubis3JitterAnimateKm;
  float adaptiveCeilKm = max(4.0f * args.cloudViewStepKm, args.nubis3AdaptiveStepKm);
  uint  iterCap = max(uint(args.cloudViewSamplesMax), 2u);
  float t = tEntry;
  float sdfStepKm = 0.0f;  // previous sample's conservative empty radius
  // Far end of the previous sample's guaranteed-empty interval, as a distance along the ray
  // (fork -- 2026-09-08, sphere-trace overshoot fix; see the skip below). tEntry = "nothing
  // proven yet", so the first iteration takes the adaptive step exactly as before.
  float tEmptyUntilKm = tEntry;
  for (uint iter = 0u; iter < iterCap && t < tExit; ++iter) {
    if (viewTransmittance < kCloudExitTransmittance) break;  // transmittance-limit exit (p.174)

    float stepKm = clamp(args.cloudViewStepKm * sqrt(t * (1.0f / kAdaptiveRefDistKm)),
                         args.nubis3AdaptiveStepKm, adaptiveCeilKm);
#if !defined(CLOUD_EMPTY_CURSOR_ADVANCE)
    if (args.nvdfStepScale > 0.0f) {
      // Inside cloud the conservative empty radius goes <= 0 and max() hands
      // control to the adaptive step — exactly the reference's hybrid.
      //
      // Anchored to the CURSOR, not to the sample (fork -- 2026-09-08, sphere-trace overshoot
      // fix). The empty radius r is measured at tSample = t + hash * step and licenses
      // [tSample - r, tSample + r]. Until now the skip was `sdfStepKm * nvdfStepScale` applied
      // from the cursor t + step, which sits (1 - hash) * step BEYOND the point r was measured
      // from -- so the next sample could land up to step * (1 - hash) - 0.05 r past the
      // guaranteed-empty boundary. For a km-scale approach skip that is hundreds of metres to a
      // kilometre INSIDE the body: the sphere trace did not converge on the surface, it
      // overshot it, by an amount that depended on the jitter and on how long the last empty
      // stretch was. The lattice march never had this problem (its skip advances the sample
      // index, so it is sample-anchored by construction); the hybrid loop decoupled the cursor
      // from the sample on 2026-07-16 and inherited it. Three consequences, all worst at high
      // extinction: the first in-cloud sample sat at a random depth of 0-500+ m with a Beer
      // segment of up to the whole skip (1-2 km, i.e. 7-13 optical depths at cloudDensity 6.7),
      // so one interior-shaded sample decided the pixel and the lit skin was never sampled --
      // faces approached across long empty stretches (undersides seen from below, tower sides
      // across gaps) rendered dark and flat; the overshoot depth followed the sphere-trace's
      // own cell structure, so its shading error had spatial contours -- the onion-shell
      // banding nvdfStepScale's tooltip already warned about; and the animated jitter turned
      // the per-frame depth lottery into speckle the EMA could only partly average
      // (565c09ef3's "density-skip flipping against a step much larger than the detail it
      // samples" is this mechanism seen from the other side).
      //
      // The safe advance from the cursor is (tSample + r) - (t + step) = r - step * (1 - hash),
      // carried across iterations as tEmptyUntilKm. With it the cursor advances 95% of its
      // remaining distance to the proven boundary each iteration -- the textbook sphere trace,
      // independent of the jitter -- and still reaches the adaptive floor in 2-3 iterations from
      // km range, so empty air costs the same as before; what changes is that the trace now
      // stops AT the bounding surface and the first in-cloud samples come from the adaptive
      // stepping through the displacement band, where they belong. Inside cloud r <= 0 keeps
      // this negative and the adaptive step wins, as before.
      stepKm = max(stepKm, (tEmptyUntilKm - t) * args.nvdfStepScale);
    }
#endif
    stepKm = min(stepKm, tExit - t);  // final-segment Beer length stays exact
    if (stepKm <= 1e-5f) break;       // FP guard: t += denormal would stall

    float hash    = t < kJitterAnimateKm ? pixelJitter : pixelJitterStatic;
    float tSample = t + hash * stepKm;
    float rayFrac = saturate((tSample - tEntry) / (tExit - tEntry));
    ++g_cloudMarchSamples;
    integrateCloudSample(tSample, stepKm, rayFrac, viewDirYUp,
                         slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm,
                         slabTypeMean, slabCoverageMean,
                         slabDensityScale,
                         typeNoiseEntry, typeNoiseExit,
                         coverageNoiseEntry, coverageNoiseExit,
                         ctx, args, accumColor, viewTransmittance,
                         sdfStepKm, sumWeightedDistKm, sumWeight, entryDistanceKm);
    // The interval this sample proved empty, in ray distance; the next iteration skips to it
    // from wherever its cursor is (see above).
    tEmptyUntilKm = tSample + sdfStepKm;
    t += stepKm;
#if defined(CLOUD_EMPTY_CURSOR_ADVANCE)
    // Advance only through the remainder of the sample-anchored empty interval.
    // Feeding this distance into a jittered sample instead re-evaluates known empty air.
    const float emptyAdvanceKm = max(tEmptyUntilKm - t, 0.0f) * saturate(args.nvdfStepScale);
    t = min(t + emptyAdvanceKm, tExit);
#endif
  }

  // Budget-exhausted tail (fork -- 2026-09-17, "distant clouds disappear behind a hard line").
  //
  // A ray that hit iterCap before tExit used to simply stop, dropping whatever cloud remained along
  // it. That is invisible while the camera is far from the slab, because only horizon-grazing rays
  // ever reach the cap. Approach the deck and it becomes the dominant case: near the slab a ray
  // spends its whole budget on the fine adaptive steps that cross the cloud in front of it, exhausts
  // the cap a few km out, and deletes everything past that point. Since the exhaustion distance is
  // roughly the same for every ray in a direction, the deletion has a sharp edge -- a hard line
  // across the sky that moves as the camera approaches, which is exactly what was reported.
  //
  // The cap dropped 256 -> 64 in 4050ab438 and the spacing 0.1 -> 0.15 km, which is what surfaced
  // this: those are the user's measured performance settings and worth keeping, but they shorten the
  // budget's reach about fourfold and so pulled the line in from beyond the horizon to inside the
  // visible sky. The cap should cost DETAIL, not cloud.
  //
  // So integrate the remainder coarsely instead of discarding it: a few long Beer-Lambert segments
  // over [t, tExit]. Four rather than one because a single sample at the tail's midpoint would let
  // one dense hit turn the entire remaining span opaque -- swapping a hard bright line for a hard
  // dark one. Only rays that actually exhausted the budget pay for it, and they pay four samples on
  // top of the cap, so the worst case grows by 4/cloudViewSamplesMax.
  if (t < tExit && viewTransmittance > kCloudExitTransmittance) {
    const uint  kTailSamples = 4u;
    const float tailStepKm   = (tExit - t) / float(kTailSamples);
    if (tailStepKm > 1e-5f) {
      for (uint tail = 0u; tail < kTailSamples; ++tail) {
        if (viewTransmittance < kCloudExitTransmittance) break;
        const float tSample = t + (float(tail) + 0.5f) * tailStepKm;
        const float rayFrac = saturate((tSample - tEntry) / max(tExit - tEntry, 1e-6f));
        ++g_cloudMarchSamples;
        integrateCloudSample(tSample, tailStepKm, rayFrac, viewDirYUp,
                             slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm,
                             slabTypeMean, slabCoverageMean,
                             slabDensityScale,
                             typeNoiseEntry, typeNoiseExit,
                             coverageNoiseEntry, coverageNoiseExit,
                             ctx, args, accumColor, viewTransmittance,
                             sdfStepKm, sumWeightedDistKm, sumWeight, entryDistanceKm);
      }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
// Echo-deck march (fork — 2026-06-21, layer-2 rework). A deliberately CHEAP
// second deck that reuses layer 1's exact density model ("the same cloud
// slab") at a higher, gapped altitude — the perf-conscious replacement for
// the old fully-independent layer-2 marchCloudSlab pass (which inherited
// layer 1's tall-cumulus step floor and tapped the layer-1-only D_sun grid).
//
// Three things make it cheaper than a second full slab march, none of which
// touch layer 1:
//   * Low step budget. Layer 1's step floor (sampleCount, ~32) is sized for
//     tall cumulus that fills the slab top-to-bottom; a thinner echo deck
//     doesn't need it. The floor/cap here are the user-adjustable
//     cloudLayer2StepFloor / cloudLayer2StepMax (default 8 / 16), so the deck
//     costs a fraction of a full second pass. Deck cost is ~linear in step
//     count, so this is the dominant win.
//   * Analytic sun shadow. The evaluator core's one remaining voxel-grid tap
//     (D_sun) is baked over the LAYER-1 slab only, so a deck at a different
//     altitude reads meaningless residual from it. We pass a cheap analytic
//     D_sun proxy instead (dSunOverride >= 0) — one fewer texture tap per
//     dense sample AND no stale-grid artifact.
//   * No moon path. A thin echo deck contributes no resolvable moonlit silver
//     lining, so the whole per-sample moon-shadow block is simply absent here
//     (the old layer-2 path zeroed the moon coefficients to the same end).
//
// Everything else is identical to marchCloudSlab on purpose: the same per-
// column density model (so the deck reads as the same kind of cloud AND keeps
// the per-column early-out that refunds the placement tap on empty steps), the
// same coverage/type control-field machinery driven by the deck's own
// mean / spread / seed knobs (= the "varied echo" — a decorrelated but
// related cloudscape via cloudLayer2NoiseSeed), and the same Beer-Lambert +
// aerial-perspective accumulation. The caller's distance clamps apply to both
// decks (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage 1: the split-march tMin/tMax clamps
// were dropped here on the reasoning that "the echo deck never needs the partition experiment", but
// the clamp isn't only for that experiment — it is also how a resolved surface hit is threaded
// through so a surface cannot receive cloud behind its own resolved point. Neither current caller
// (cloud_render.comp.slang, cloud_secondary_lut.comp.slang) passes a nonzero clamp today, so this is
// a signature-only change with no render delta yet; it exists so a future depth-aware caller
// (Stage 4) does not have to re-plumb the deck separately from layer 1).
// =============================================================================
void marchEchoDeck(vec3   viewDirYUp,
                   float  pixelJitter,
                   float  slabAltitudeKm,
                   float  slabThicknessKm,
                   float  slabTypeMean,
                   float  slabCoverageMean,
                   float  slabDensityScale,
                   float  slabNoiseSeedOffset,
                   float  tMinClamp,
                   float  tMaxClamp,
                   CloudShadeContext ctx,
                   AtmosphereArgs args,
                   inout vec3  accumColor,
                   inout float viewTransmittance,
                   // Depth-companion accumulators (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration
                   // Stage 4a) — same running sums marchCloudSlab threads through
                   // integrateCloudSample, so the echo deck's contribution folds into the same
                   // combined mean-depth estimate. entryDistanceKm is threaded here too (fork --
                   // 2026-09-06, occlusion accuracy; layer 2 deliberately had no such parameter
                   // before). Once entry means "first density hit" rather than "where layer 1's
                   // slab started", layer 2 has to be able to set it: a ray crossing an empty
                   // patch of layer 1 into cirrus would otherwise keep the -1 sentinel and have
                   // its perfectly real layer-2 cloud zeroed by loadCurrentCloudOpacity.
                   inout float sumWeightedDistKm,
                   inout float sumWeight,
                   inout float entryDistanceKm) {
  // Slab intersection via the shared helper (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration
  // Stage 1) — see cloudSlabSpan's comment and marchCloudSlab's identical swap above.
  float tEntry, tExit;
  // Piece 0 only: the echo deck is a deliberately cheap single-crossing approximation, and its
  // underside is thin cirrus rather than the primary deck's visible floor.
  if (!cloudSlabSpan(viewDirYUp, ctx.planetCenterKm, ctx.planetRadiusKm,
                     slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm, /*spanPiece=*/0, tEntry, tExit)) return;
  tEntry = max(tEntry, tMinClamp);
  if (tMaxClamp > 0.0f) {
    tExit = min(tExit, tMaxClamp);
  }
  if (tEntry >= tExit || tExit <= 0.0f) return;
  tEntry = max(tEntry, 0.0f);

  // Low step budget — the echo deck's headline perf lever, user-adjustable via
  // cloudLayer2StepFloor / cloudLayer2StepMax (decoupled from layer 1's
  // cloudViewSamples / cloudViewSamplesMax, and defaulted far lower: 8 / 16).
  // Honor the same cloudViewStepKm length target as layer 1 (so horizon-grazing
  // rays still step at a sane density), clamped to the deck's own floor/cap.
  // kEchoStepHardCap is a GPU-hang backstop, NOT a tuning knob: if the
  // cloudLayer2Step* CB fields ever read garbage (e.g. a constant-buffer
  // misalignment), an unclamped step count would spin the march into a device
  // hang = solid-black frame. Cap well above the option's legitimate max (128)
  // so it never bites real values but bounds the worst case.
  const uint kEchoStepHardCap = 256u;
  uint echoFloor = clamp(args.cloudLayer2StepFloor, 1u, kEchoStepHardCap);
  uint echoMax   = clamp(max(args.cloudLayer2StepMax, echoFloor), echoFloor, kEchoStepHardCap);
  uint effSampleCount = echoFloor;
  if (args.cloudViewStepKm > 0.001f) {
    uint wanted = uint(ceil((tExit - tEntry) / args.cloudViewStepKm));
    effSampleCount = clamp(wanted, echoFloor, echoMax);
  }
  float stepLen = (tExit - tEntry) / float(effSampleCount);

  // Decouple the moon-shadow extinction from the main slider too. ctx is built
  // once from the MAIN args (moonBaseSigma = 2 × main cloudDensity); rebuild it
  // here from the deck's decoupled args.cloudDensity (= args2) so the deck's
  // moonlit self-shadow tracks the deck's own density, matching the sun path.
  ctx.moonBaseSigma = 2.0f * args.cloudDensity;

  // Coverage / type control fields — hoisted entry/exit lerp, same structure as
  // marchCloudSlab. The slabNoiseSeedOffset (cloudLayer2NoiseSeed) shifts the
  // 2D hash so the deck generates a decorrelated-but-related field at the
  // same XZ; a seed shift is geometry-free (a position shift would fight the
  // length()-based spherical altitude math in computeCloudHeightFraction).
  //
  // Coverage spread is forced to 0 for the deck (see marchCloudLayers), so the
  // coverage field collapses to a uniform slabCoverageMean and the coverage taps
  // below fall out of the per-step blend — that's what removed the coverage
  // "tiling". Type still varies via the plain single-octave field.
  vec3 entryPosCF = args.cameraWorldPosYUpKm + viewDirYUp * tEntry;
  vec3 exitPosCF  = args.cameraWorldPosYUpKm + viewDirYUp * tExit;
  vec2 entryXZ = vec2(entryPosCF.x, entryPosCF.z);
  vec2 exitXZ  = vec2(exitPosCF.x,  exitPosCF.z);
  float typeNoiseEntry = 0.5f;
  float typeNoiseExit = 0.5f;
  float coverageNoiseEntry = 0.5f;
  float coverageNoiseExit = 0.5f;
  if (args.cloudTypeSpread != 0.0f) {
    typeNoiseEntry = cloudControlField2D(entryXZ * args.cloudTypeNoiseScale, 200.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
    typeNoiseExit = cloudControlField2D(exitXZ * args.cloudTypeNoiseScale, 200.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
  }
  if (args.cloudCoverageSpread != 0.0f) {
    coverageNoiseEntry = cloudControlField2D(entryXZ * args.cloudCoverageNoiseScale, 250.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
    coverageNoiseExit = cloudControlField2D(exitXZ * args.cloudCoverageNoiseScale, 250.0f + slabNoiseSeedOffset);
  }

  // Echo-deck Nubis3 conversion (fork — legacy retirement, 2026-07-16). The
  // deck now samples the SAME NVDF/detail model as layer 1 ("the same cloud
  // slab" idea, made literal) with the density-FIELD position offset by a
  // seed-keyed XZ shift — a decorrelated-but-related copy of the layer-1
  // bodies at the deck's altitude. Only the field position shifts; hf,
  // lighting, and aerial terms stay on the TRUE sample position (a position
  // shift there would fight the length()-based spherical altitude math —
  // the reason the control fields above use a seed shift instead).
  vec3 fieldOffsetKm = vec3(31.7f, 0.0f, -17.3f) * slabNoiseSeedOffset;

  for (uint i = 0u; i < effSampleCount; ++i) {
    if (viewTransmittance < kCloudExitTransmittance) break;  // same exit as layer 1

    float t = tEntry + stepLen * (float(i) + pixelJitter);
    vec3 samplePos = args.cameraWorldPosYUpKm + viewDirYUp * t;

    float rayFrac = (float(i) + pixelJitter) / float(effSampleCount);
    float typeNoise     = mix(typeNoiseEntry,     typeNoiseExit,     rayFrac);
    float coverageNoise = mix(coverageNoiseEntry, coverageNoiseExit, rayFrac);
    float typeLocal     = saturate(slabTypeMean     + (typeNoise     - 0.5f) * args.cloudTypeSpread);
    float coverageLocal = saturate(slabCoverageMean + (coverageNoise - 0.5f) * args.cloudCoverageSpread);

    float hf = computeCloudHeightFractionC(samplePos, ctx.planetCenterWorldKm, ctx.planetRadiusKm,
                                           slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm);
    float nubisProfile;
    float detailSigned;
    float nubisSdfKm;
    float nubisSdfStepKm;
    float density = sampleCloudDensityNubis3(samplePos + fieldOffsetKm, hf, typeLocal,
                                             coverageLocal, slabDensityScale,
                                             /*cameraDistKm=*/t,
                                             args.cloudDetailLodEnable != 0u ? stepLen : 0.0f, args,
                                             AtmosphereCloudNvdfSdf, AtmosphereCloudDetailNoise3D,
                                             AtmosphereCloudNoise3DSampler,
                                             /*coarseOd=*/false,
                                             nubisProfile, detailSigned, nubisSdfKm,
                                             nubisSdfStepKm);
    if (density < 0.001f) {
      // Same SDF empty-space index skip as layer 1's fixed-step march.
      if (args.nvdfStepScale > 0.0f) {
        float skipKm = nubisSdfStepKm * args.nvdfStepScale;
        if (skipKm > stepLen) {
          uint advance = uint(min(floor(skipKm / stepLen), 4096.0f));
          i += advance - 1u;  // the loop's ++i completes the advance
        }
      }
      continue;
    }

#if !CLOUD_PROFILE_DENSITY_ONLY
    // Analytic sun-shadow optical depth in place of the layer-1-only D_sun
    // grid tap: depth grows toward the deck base (1 - hf) and scales with
    // the deck's own thickness × density. Cheap, artifact-free, and good
    // enough for a thin deck where the forward-scatter / silver-lining cue
    // dominates the look anyway. (>= 0, so the evaluator skips the grid.)
    float dSunProxy = (1.0f - hf) * slabThicknessKm * slabDensityScale;

    // downTau = 0: no underside darkening on a thin high deck (the D_ambient
    // grid covers layer 1 only, and cirrus undersides are effectively unshaded).
    float cloudSdfM = clamp(nubisSdfKm * 1000.0f, -args.cloudMsSdfDepth * 4.0f, 0.0f);
    NubisCubedLighting lighting = evalNubisCubedSampleCore(
        samplePos, density, nubisProfile, cloudSdfM, /*downTau=*/0.0f, detailSigned,
        ctx, args, /*isZUp=*/false, /*dSunOverride=*/dSunProxy);

    // Per-sample moon direct light (fork — 2026-06-21). The echo deck is the
    // same cloud model as layer 1, so under a bright moon it must catch the
    // same silver lining / direct scatter — without this the upper deck read
    // flat while the lower deck was rim-lit. Same Beer-Lambert single-octave
    // shadow + Wrenninge-smoothed phase as marchCloudSlab, gated to dense night
    // samples; the shadow tap is the slab-aware variant so the deck self-
    // shadows against its OWN altitude band (see sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth_
    // localSlab). The if-gate skips the whole block by day (moon coeffs zero).
    const float kMoonShadowSkipDensity = 0.05f;
    vec3 moonDirectLight = vec3(0.0f);
    if (ctx.moonActive) {
      float moonOpticalDepth = 0.0f;
#if !CLOUD_PROFILE_NO_MOON_SHADOWS
      if (density >= kMoonShadowSkipDensity) {
        moonOpticalDepth = sampleCloudSunOpticalDepth_localSlab(
            samplePos, ctx.combinedMoonDir, typeLocal, coverageLocal,
            slabAltitudeKm, slabThicknessKm, slabDensityScale, fieldOffsetKm,
            ctx.planetCenterWorldKm, ctx.planetRadiusKm, args);
      }
#endif
      float moonShadow = exp(-moonOpticalDepth * ctx.moonBaseSigma);
      // Ray-constant phase-octave sum — see cloudShadeContextBeginRay.
      moonDirectLight = (ctx.combinedMoonDiffuseCoeff
                       + ctx.combinedMoonPhaseAmt * ctx.msMoonAniso) * moonShadow;
    }

    // Same thin-edge ambient fade + composite as marchCloudSlab. nightLight is
    // added uncolored so the deck picks up airglow at night like layer 1.
    float ambientThinFade = args.cloudEdgeAmbientFade > 1e-5f
                          ? smoothstep(0.0f, args.cloudEdgeAmbientFade, density)
                          : 1.0f;
    vec3 sampleColor = args.cloudColor
                     * (lighting.direct + lighting.ambient * ambientThinFade + moonDirectLight)
                     + ctx.nightLight;

#if CLOUD_MARCH_LIGHTNING
    // Lightning flash glow (fork — 2026-07-14): a low strike faintly lights
    // the high deck too — same term as layer 1, distance does the fading.
    if (args.lightningFlashIntensity > 0.0f) {
      sampleColor += evalLightningFlash(samplePos, args);
    }
#endif

#else
    vec3 sampleColor = vec3(1.0f);
#endif

    float effectiveDensity = density * args.cloudDensity;
    float stepTransmittance = exp(-effectiveDensity * stepLen);
    vec3 stepRadiance = sampleColor * (1.0f - stepTransmittance);

    float aerialT_haze = exp(-args.cloudAerialHazePerKm * t);
    float aerialT_fade = exp(-args.cloudAerialFadePerKm * t);
    // sampleWeight named explicitly here (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration Stage 4a)
    // so the depth-companion accumulation below can reuse it -- the exact same value implicit in
    // the accumColor update, computed before viewTransmittance is mutated. Purely a refactor of the
    // existing expression: the accumColor write is byte-identical to before.
    float sampleWeight = viewTransmittance * aerialT_haze * (1.0f - stepTransmittance);
    accumColor += stepRadiance * viewTransmittance * aerialT_haze;
    // Same first-density-hit narrowing layer 1 does inside integrateCloudSample. This site is
    // already past this layer's own `density < 0.001f` gate, so reaching it means real material.
    if (entryDistanceKm < 0.0f || t < entryDistanceKm) {
      entryDistanceKm = t;
    }
    sumWeightedDistKm += t * sampleWeight;
    sumWeight         += sampleWeight;
    viewTransmittance *= mix(1.0f, stepTransmittance, aerialT_fade);
  }
}

// =============================================================================
// Both-layer march wrapper — the layer-1 cumulus slab plus the optional
// layer-2 echo deck. Shared so the secondary-ray LUT bake composites the
// deck identically. Layer 1 is the full marchCloudSlab; layer 2 is the lean
// marchEchoDeck (see its header). Front-to-back ordering: layer 1 (lower,
// nearer) marches first so its residual transmittance composites the higher
// deck behind it.
// =============================================================================
void marchCloudLayers(vec3  viewDirYUp,
                      float pixelJitter,
                      float pixelJitterStatic,
                      float tMinClamp,
                      float tMaxClamp,
                      uint  sampleCount,
                      CloudShadeContext ctx,
                      AtmosphereArgs args,
                      inout vec3  accumColor,
                      inout float viewTransmittance,
                      // Depth-companion outputs (fork — 2026-09-05, world-space cloud migration
                      // Stage 4a; redefined 2026-09-06, occlusion
                      // accuracy). entryDistanceKm: the nearest distance (km) at which EITHER
                      // layer found actual cloud material, or -1.0f if neither did -- an empty
                      // slab and a slab with no cloud along this ray are deliberately the same
                      // answer now, which is what lets cloudDepthVisibility stop fogging surfaces
                      // that sit in front of the nearest real cloud.
                      // meanCloudDepthKm: the transmittance-weighted mean distance
                      // (km) through whatever cloud material both layers found along the ray, or
                      // 0.0f if none (the running weight never left ~0). Only cloud_render.comp.slang
                      // consumes these; cloud_secondary_lut.comp.slang passes throwaway locals --
                      // see that file's call site.
                      out float entryDistanceKm,
                      out float meanCloudDepthKm) {
  // Per-ray shading precompute (fork — 2026-07-30, perf). Fills the
  // view-direction-dependent half of the context ONCE, replacing the same work
  // repeated at every march sample. Both layers share it: every term here is
  // identical under the layer-2 args override (which only touches cloudDensity,
  // cloudThickness, cloudTypeMean, cloudColor and the spreads — none of which
  // feed these fields).
  ctx = cloudShadeContextBeginRay(ctx, viewDirYUp, args);

  // Depth-companion accumulators (fork — 2026-09-05, Stage 4a), initialized to the "nothing found
  // yet" sentinels documented above and folded into a single mean-depth value once both layers
  // have run (see the divide at the bottom of this function).
  entryDistanceKm = -1.0f;
  float sumWeightedDistKm = 0.0f;
  float sumWeight = 0.0f;

  // Both slab crossings, near then far, so accumulation stays front-to-back (fork -- 2026-09-06,
  // fly-through polish). The second call returns immediately unless the eye is above the deck base
  // looking down through it, which is the only configuration with two crossings -- so this costs a
  // span test per pixel in every other case, and renders the deck's far underside in that one.
  for (int spanPiece = 0; spanPiece < 2; ++spanPiece) {
    if (viewTransmittance < kCloudExitTransmittance) {
      break;
    }
    marchCloudSlab(viewDirYUp, pixelJitter, pixelJitterStatic,
                   args.cloudAltitude, args.cloudThickness,
                   args.cloudTypeMean, args.cloudCoverageMean,
                   /*slabDensityScale=*/1.0f,
                   /*slabNoiseSeedOffset=*/0.0f,
                   tMinClamp, tMaxClamp,
                   spanPiece,
                   sampleCount, ctx, args,
                   accumColor, viewTransmittance,
                   entryDistanceKm, sumWeightedDistKm, sumWeight);
  }

  // Layer 2 echo deck — same slab model at a gapped altitude, marched cheaply.
  // Reads its own coverage/type spread via an args copy so the shared density
  // sampler applies the deck's variation; its mean/density/seed come through
  // marchEchoDeck's slab params directly.
  if (args.cloudLayer2Enable != 0u && viewTransmittance > kCloudExitTransmittance) {
    AtmosphereArgs args2 = args;
    // Deck coverage spread is forced to 0: the spread amplified the single-
    // octave Worley coverage lattice into a fine tiled/stippled texture at the
    // deck's distance (the cells are ~3 km, tiny in angle from far away). With
    // spread 0 the deck's coverage collapses to a uniform slabCoverageMean
    // sheet — no coverage field, no tiling. (The cloudLayer2CoverageSpread
    // option was removed; type spread is still available.)
    args2.cloudCoverageSpread = 0.0f;
    args2.cloudTypeSpread     = args.cloudLayer2TypeSpread;
    // Decouple the deck's density from the MAIN cloudDensity slider. cloudDensity
    // is the shared extinction coefficient (used by the march, the lighting
    // sigma/downTau, and the SDF proxy), so moving the main Density slider
    // otherwise scaled BOTH decks — most visibly the deck's opacity. Pin the
    // deck's extinction to a fixed reference equal to cloudDensity's 1.8 default,
    // so (a) the main slider no longer bleeds into the deck and (b) the deck look
    // is unchanged at the default main density. The deck's OWN opacity stays on
    // cloudLayer2DensityScale (the sampler's slabDensityScale). marchEchoDeck
    // threads args2 through the sampler + lighting + moon path, so this decouples
    // the deck's density consistently, not just its march extinction. Keep this
    // matched to cloudDensity's RTX_OPTION default if that ever changes.
    const float kEchoDeckExtinction = 1.8f;
    args2.cloudDensity = kEchoDeckExtinction;
    // The shared lighting evaluator (evalNubisCubedSampleCore and the
    // sampler's SDF/profile inputs) reads args.cloudThickness
    // and args.cloudTypeMean DIRECTLY — they don't get the deck's slab params
    // the way the density sampler does. Left unset, the deck's SDF proxy,
    // dim profile and underside optical depth would key on layer 1's thickness
    // and type instead of the deck's own. Point them at the deck's values so
    // the deck's shape-driven lighting matches its geometry, not layer 1's.
    // (Coverage already flows through coverageLocal, so it needs no override.)
    args2.cloudThickness = args.cloudLayer2Thickness;
    args2.cloudTypeMean  = args.cloudLayer2TypeMean;
    // Independent deck albedo — the one look knob split out from layer 1 (the
    // march composite multiplies all scatter terms by args.cloudColor). Defaults
    // to the same near-white, so this is a no-op until the user tints it.
    args2.cloudColor     = args.cloudLayer2Color;
    marchEchoDeck(viewDirYUp, pixelJitter,
                  args.cloudLayer2Altitude, args.cloudLayer2Thickness,
                  args.cloudLayer2TypeMean, args.cloudLayer2CoverageMean,
                  args.cloudLayer2DensityScale,
                  args.cloudLayer2NoiseSeed,
                  tMinClamp, tMaxClamp,
                  ctx, args2,
                  accumColor, viewTransmittance,
                  sumWeightedDistKm, sumWeight, entryDistanceKm);
  }

  // Report transmittance on the scale whose zero is the march's own exit threshold (fork --
  // 2026-09-08, disc leak; the full reasoning, and the two rejected alternatives, are at
  // kCloudExitTransmittance).
  //
  //   T' = max(0, (T - e) / (1 - e)),   C' = C / (1 - e),   e = kCloudExitTransmittance
  //
  // Every ray that broke on the transmittance limit was below e when it broke, so it lands at
  // exactly 0: no background reaches a pixel the march called opaque, at any view angle. Every
  // other ray moves continuously with it -- T' is monotone in T and exactly 1 at T = 1 -- so the
  // threshold draws no contour anywhere, which is the whole point.
  //
  // The radiance takes the SAME factor, which is what makes this "the same cloud, 1% more opaque"
  // rather than a 1%-of-background darkening. The energy the shift takes out of the background is
  // (T - T') x Lbar, where Lbar = accumColor / (1 - T) is the mean cloud radiance the march has
  // already measured; substituting T - T' = e(1 - T)/(1 - e) collapses that to exactly
  // C x e/(1 - e), i.e. C' = C/(1 - e). So the pair stays a valid premultiplied (radiance,
  // transmittance) description of a cloud, and the composite stays exact in the thin limit instead
  // of running 1% dark. Both are ~1% effects on cloud that is not near-opaque, and invisible
  // there; the leak they remove is 4-10x the cloud's own radiance on the pixels that ARE.
  //
  // Here rather than in the loops: once per ray, after BOTH slab crossings and the echo deck have
  // contributed (transmittance is multiplicative, so a per-loop shift would compound), and both
  // callers -- the screen pass and the secondary-LUT bake -- inherit it identically.
  const float kExitRescale = 1.0f / (1.0f - kCloudExitTransmittance);
  accumColor        *= kExitRescale;
  viewTransmittance  = max(0.0f, (viewTransmittance - kCloudExitTransmittance) * kExitRescale);

  // Finalize the depth companion (fork — 2026-09-05, Stage 4a): both layers have now contributed
  // whatever weight they found, so this is the one point where the running sums become a mean.
  // sumWeight stays ~0 only when neither layer put any cloud material on the ray (both slabs
  // missed, or every sample density-gated out) -- meanCloudDepthKm is meaningless there, so it
  // reports the "no cloud" sentinel instead of dividing by (near) zero.
  meanCloudDepthKm = (sumWeight > 1e-6f) ? (sumWeightedDistKm / sumWeight) : 0.0f;
}
